n research is done to test hypotheses and theories about how and why phenomena operate as they do, then the primary purpose of such research is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Descriptive"},{"label":"B","text":"Predictive"},{"label":"C","text":"Explanatory"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d63389bbcf73babefa0de06b65365027c49a6b79"},{"id":"q-cb91fbda955c","stem":"Active immunization is available against all of the following diseases except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"tuberculosis;"},{"label":"B","text":"smallpox;"},{"label":"C","text":"poliomyelitis;"},{"label":"D","text":"malaria;"},{"label":"E","text":"yellow fever. Notes Questions:"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"6586cdbebac0add06a38d45b11cfc7f610459c99"},{"id":"q-3d2322ff4255","stem":"An effect size indicator is a statistical measure of the strength of a relationship.","type":"True / False","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"True"},{"label":"B","text":"False"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-789c62b046f4","stem":"If a finding is statistically significant one must also interpret the data, calculate an effect size indicator, and make an assessment of practical significance.","type":"Calculation","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"True"},{"label":"B","text":"False"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-06517155f6cc","stem":"The p-value used in statistical significance testing should be used to assess how strong a relationship is. For example, if relationship A has a p=.04 and relationship B has a p=.03 then you can conclude that relationship B is stronger than relationship A.","type":"True / False","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"True"},{"label":"B","text":"False SHORT NOTES QUESTIONS"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-04511d8642c4","stem":"1. Select the best answer.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") Randomized controlled trials are considered the “gold standard” of epidemiology study designs because randomization guarantees comparability of study arms."},{"label":"B","text":") A weakness of randomized controlled trials is that they are prone to misclassification of exposure status."},{"label":"C","text":") It is difficult to perform intention-to-treat analyses with randomized controlled trials."},{"label":"D","text":") Randomized controlled trials are observation studies because study arms are observed for outcomes."},{"label":"E","text":") Randomized controlled trials are not an effective study design for assessing rare outcomes. 1."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7c799933b4dd","stem":"2. Investigators know the assignments of subjects in a randomized controlled trial. What might be done to help avoid random (non-differential) misclassification of outcomes in the trial? Select the best answer.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") Blind the subjects to the purpose of the trial."},{"label":"B","text":") Have a precise and well-defined definition of the outcome."},{"label":"C","text":") Increase the study’s power."},{"label":"D","text":") Assure that the study arms are comparable by performing an adjustment if necessary."},{"label":"E","text":") Ask a blinded outcome assessment committee to assess outcomes."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8d4a98d76c2f","stem":"3. Select the correct statement concerning retrospective and prospective studies. 2.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") Retrospective studies are less subject to misclassification of outcome status compared to prospective studies."},{"label":"B","text":") Retrospective studies are less subject to misclassification of exposure status compared to prospective studies."},{"label":"C","text":") Retrospective studies are usually less costly to conduct compared to prospective studies."},{"label":"D","text":") Relative risks can’t be calculated in retrospective studies."},{"label":"E","text":") Multiple outcomes can’t be assessed in retrospective studies. 3. 4. 5. 6. 7. 8."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-090325dc70ad","stem":"4. Select the correct statement: 10.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The p-value is always calculated under the assumption that the null hypothesis is true."},{"label":"B","text":"The p-value is a good estimate of how likely the null hypothesis is incorrect."},{"label":"C","text":"The lower the p-value, the more likely the study results will benefit patients."},{"label":"D","text":"The p-value is a good estimate of how likely the null hypothesis is correct."},{"label":"E","text":"The p-value is calculated under the assumption that the alternative hypothesis is true."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fe7f75e97a13","stem":"Researchers are concerned that smoking could be a significant confounder in a randomized controlled trial they are planning to conduct. Select the best statement concerning the researchers’ desire to avoid confounding by smoking","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") The researchers should match study arms for cigarette smoking to eliminate smoking as a potential confounder."},{"label":"B","text":". The researchers should develop a protocol to help assure that the loss to follow-up is less than 10%."},{"label":"C","text":") The researchers should change their study design such that only smokers are allowed to participate in the trial."},{"label":"D","text":") The researchers should compare their crude rate to the smoking-adjusted rate when the study is completed."},{"label":"E","text":"The researchers should compare their crude rate to the smoking-adjusted rate when the study is completed to assess for interaction. 1."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e95bb62b4c20","stem":"Select the correct statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") The shapes of normal distributions can vary, reflective of different variances."},{"label":"B","text":") 5% of the variables in a normal distribution are above the positive second standard deviation."},{"label":"C","text":") Data in a skewed distribution can be transformed to normally distributed data by multivariate adjustment."},{"label":"D","text":") If the standard deviation in a normal distribution is 16, the variance is four."},{"label":"E","text":") As a general statement, data in a normal distribution are more useful clinically than data in a skewed distribution. 2."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8c322ed680a0cae1eddd73b928d018339f3a84bb"},{"id":"q-87043a078948","stem":"Researchers want to assess if there is an association between cocaine use and congestive heart failure. Select the correct statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") The researchers should perform a randomized controlled trial."},{"label":"B","text":") The researchers should be concerned about recall bias if they choose to perform a retrospective cohort study."},{"label":"C","text":") By strictly defining congestive heart failure, the researchers should avoid confounding."},{"label":"D","text":") The researchers should avoid selection bias by assuring comparability of study arms."},{"label":"E","text":") The researchers should be concerned about reporting bias if they conduct a case control study. 1."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-91ba7e9a134a","stem":"A study published in a medical journal reported that there was no association between eating fish and the risk of a heart attack. Two months later, the same medical journal published another study that reported there was an association between eating fish and the risk of a heart attack. In both studies the subjects’ responses were either yes or no, that is they either eat fish or they do not eat fish. Which of the following statements is a possible explanation for the differing conclusions?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") The exclusionary criteria were different for the two studies."},{"label":"B","text":") A Type I error occurred in the first published study."},{"label":"C","text":") There was interaction by fish eating in the second published study."},{"label":"D","text":") The second published study had insufficient power."},{"label":"E","text":") A Type II error occurred in the second published study."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e79f8cc01588","stem":"Researchers want to assess if there is an association between cigarette smoking and strokes. Of the following, which is the best study design to assess for this association?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") Case series"},{"label":"B","text":") Cross-Sectional study"},{"label":"C","text":") Prospective cohort study"},{"label":"D","text":") Randomized controlled trial"},{"label":"E","text":") Intervention study 1."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3c749d60be5914fcf61ee9d775b6c28252a57820"},{"id":"q-053e1dab5944","stem":"The informed consent process requires all of the following except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") The subjects must understand the potential pros and cons of participating in the study"},{"label":"B","text":") A description of alternative treatment, if available"},{"label":"C","text":") A statement regarding the subject’s financial compensation for non-minor injuries"},{"label":"D","text":") A statement indicating the subject can change study arms upon the subject’s request"},{"label":"E","text":") A detailed statement of what is expected of the subject during the study. 2."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-76fbf2477158","stem":"Select the correct statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") A study result that is statistically significant may not lead to improved patient outcomes."},{"label":"B","text":") Clinical significance only pertains to a Type II error while statistical significance pertains to the relationship of the calculated p-value to alpha."},{"label":"C","text":") Study results that are not statistically significant can’t be clinically significant."},{"label":"D","text":") Statistical significance and clinical significance essentially mean the same thing."},{"label":"E","text":") The power must be set at 80% or higher to assure clinical significance of study results. 1. 3."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8c322ed680a0cae1eddd73b928d018339f3a84bb"},{"id":"q-85df6e311475","stem":"A researcher is told that she needs 90 subjects to attain 80% power to detect a true difference of at least 20% between two study arms. Select the correct statement: 1.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") She should reduce the number of subjects to 80 to reduce the probability of a Type II error."},{"label":"B","text":") She should set alpha at 0.01 and not 0.05 to reduce the probability of a Type II error."},{"label":"C","text":") She would need less than 90 subjects to detect a true difference of at least 15% between the two study arms, keeping the power at 80%."},{"label":"D","text":") She would need less than 90 subjects to have 90% power to detect a true difference of at least 20% between the two study arms."},{"label":"E","text":") She would need less than 90 subjects to have 80% power to detect a true difference of at least 25% between the two study arms. 1. 2. 3. 4. 5."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-efeed2bc4b28","stem":"Test selection criteria for a widespread screening program should include each of the following except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") The test should be inexpensive and available."},{"label":"B","text":") It should not be difficult to perform the test."},{"label":"C","text":") The test results should be reliable and accurate."},{"label":"D","text":") The screening test must be as good as the gold standard test."},{"label":"E","text":") The test should be of low risk to patients."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a23c693a0ddb4bc6f34f4aa166f42da342d2f85a"},{"id":"q-c4e7590527a2","stem":"Attributable risk","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") is the amount or proportion of the disease that can be attributed to a specific exposure"},{"label":"B","text":") can be used to calculate disease prevalence if the average duration of disease is known"},{"label":"C","text":") is a measurement of excess disease if the non-exposed remain non-exposed"},{"label":"D","text":") is the relative risk in the exposed minus the relative risk in the non-exposed"},{"label":"E","text":") is the prevalence of disease in the exposed minus the prevalence of disease in the non-exposed 1."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3210feea2863c517c81bf37643faec3945d0611e"},{"id":"q-6dc3d23d6e1d","stem":"Select the correct statement regarding Disease X","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") The predictive value negative of a test for Disease X will increase if the prevalence of Disease X decreases."},{"label":"B","text":") A test for Disease X that is 100% sensitive will have no false positive results."},{"label":"C","text":") A test for Disease X that is 100% specific will have no true negative results."},{"label":"D","text":") even if the prevalence of Disease X changes, the predictive value positive will not change."},{"label":"E","text":") A test yielding a 100% predictive value result for Disease X will have no false negative results."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bff965c17d8c","stem":"Researchers are interested in assessing a possible association between living in the space station for six months and a reduction of bone mineral density. The best study design to assess this possible association is: 1.","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") a randomized controlled trial"},{"label":"B","text":") a case control study"},{"label":"C","text":") a case series"},{"label":"D","text":") an ecological study"},{"label":"E","text":") a prospective cohort study 2."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6dfbb767e9d5","stem":"Recall bias occurs when","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") researchers in a case control study ask more probing questions of cases than controls."},{"label":"B","text":") researchers are not blinded and they recall which subjects were cases and which subjects were controls."},{"label":"C","text":") subjects in one arm of a randomized controlled trial recall their treatment compliance better than subjects in the other arm of the randomized controlled trial."},{"label":"D","text":") cases and controls in a case control study know their disease (outcome) status and differentially report their exposure histories."},{"label":"E","text":") the exposed and non-exposed subjects in a cohort study know their exposure status and differentially recall their exposure histories."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8d110cff5671","stem":"A parent calls the local Department of Public Health concerned that several children in his small community have been diagnosed with myocarditis, an inflammation of the heart muscle. After recording the information, one of the very first things the public health official should do is: (Select the best answer.) 1.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") Gather information from sources in the small community to determine if there is a greater than expected number of cases of myocarditis."},{"label":"B","text":") Conduct active surveillance by capturing ticks from area woods to determine the prevalence of the bacteria that causes Lyme Disease."},{"label":"C","text":") Conduct a case control study to determine if cases were more likely than controls to have eaten in the nearby fast-food restaurant."},{"label":"D","text":") Conduct a prospective cohort study to determine if exposure to wooded areas is associated with the development of myocarditis."},{"label":"E","text":") In the interest of public safety, immediately inform the media that there may be an epidemic of a potentially serious disease."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b66c3d1170af","stem":"Height is normally distributed in Town A. Researchers randomly select 50 subjects from Town A and calculate their mean height and its 95% confidence interval to make an inference about the true average height in Town A. Select the best statement","type":"Calculation","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Biostatistics","topicOriginal":"Biostatistics","subtopic":"Statistical Inference","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") Confounding could lead to an erroneous conclusion about Town A’s true average height."},{"label":"B","text":") The sample size of 50 is too small to calculate a 95% confidence interval on the mean."},{"label":"C","text":") If the researchers had drawn a sample size of 100 rather than 50, the 95% confidence interval around the mean would be narrower."},{"label":"D","text":") It is not appropriate to use a 95% confidence interval on the sample mean to make an inference about the true mean of Town A."},{"label":"E","text":") Interviewer bias could lead to an erroneous conclusion about Town A’s true average height."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Biostatistics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8805efaa176f","stem":"Researchers want to make an inference about the average serum (blood) cholesterol levels of students in college B. They will do this by randomly sampling 500 students from college B. The researchers want to be absolutely certain that they sample 250 male students and 250 female students. The researchers should: (Select the best statement.)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Biostatistics","topicOriginal":"Biostatistics","subtopic":"Statistical Inference","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") calculate a 50% confidence interval on the sample mean."},{"label":"B","text":") use the methods of stratified sampling."},{"label":"C","text":") conduct a randomized controlled trial to help assure comparability of men and women."},{"label":"D","text":") randomly select 500 subjects, and if there are 252 men and 248 women, discard the men with the highest two serum cholesterol levels and hand-select two female students."},{"label":"E","text":") perform a stratified t-test on the average serum cholesterol levels of the males vs. the females. 1. 2. 3. 4."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Biostatistics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7f10571682e8","stem":"8 extends from 0.6 to 0.9. Alpha was set at 0.05. Select the best statement: 1.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") Vegetarians were 80% less likely to develop heart disease over 30 years vs. the non-vegetarians."},{"label":"B","text":") The relative risk of 0.8 is not statistically significant as the 95% confidence interval contains the value 0.8."},{"label":"C","text":") Vegetarians were 20% less likely to develop heart disease over 30 years vs. non-vegetarians."},{"label":"D","text":") The study had 95% power to detect a true difference of 20% or more."},{"label":"E","text":") The researchers should have calculated an odds ratio rather than a relative risk."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9a0eb0b3126c","stem":"Researchers want to determine if the proportion of men from town A who smoke cigarettes is different than the proportion of women from town A who smoke cigarettes. The researchers randomly select 1,000 men and 1,200 women from town A and they ask them if they smoke cigarettes. Subjects are only permitted to answer yes or no to the question. Select the best statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") Smoking could be a confounder in this study."},{"label":"B","text":") Gender could be a confounder in this study."},{"label":"C","text":") The study likely has poor internal validity as there are more women in the study than men."},{"label":"D","text":") The researchers should test their hypothesis with a two-sample t-test."},{"label":"E","text":") The researchers should test their hypothesis with a chi-square test."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4ebb4f304a86","stem":"Researchers conduct a randomized controlled trial comparing subjects on Med A vs. Med B in the prevention of strokes. They determine that 200 subjects, 100 subjects in each arm, are required to have a power of 90% to detect a true difference of 5% or more. One percent of the subjects on Med A developed a stroke while 4% of the subjects on Med B developed a stroke. Alpha was set at 0.05 and the resulting P-value was 0.07. Select the best statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") The researchers should reject the null hypothesis."},{"label":"B","text":") Given the null hypothesis is correct, the probability of obtaining a difference of 5% or more due to chance alone is 7%."},{"label":"C","text":") Given the alternative hypothesis is correct, the probability of obtaining a difference of 5% or more due to chance alone is 7%."},{"label":"D","text":") Given the null hypothesis is correct, the probability of obtaining a difference of 3% or more due to chance alone is 90%."},{"label":"E","text":") Given the null hypothesis is correct, the probability of obtaining a difference of 3% or more due to chance alone is 7%. 28 Investigators examine data from police records to identify crash factors associated with a driver fatality vs. a driver non- fatality. The crash factors they consider are driver blood alcohol level, driver age and driver use of the seat belt. This is an example of a:"},{"label":"A","text":") case control study"},{"label":"B","text":") retrospective cohort study"},{"label":"C","text":") case series study"},{"label":"D","text":") prospective cohort study"},{"label":"E","text":") large case report study"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c54531d7a0f7","stem":"German Measles is a potentially dangerous disease in adults. It spreads quickly unless health officials intervene immediately. The Department of Public Health (DPH) requires physicians by law to report all cases of adult German measles to them by phone. A 25-year-old graduate student reports to Dr. Mtweve in a college infirmary with symptoms and a rash that suggest German measles, but the definitive blood test won’t be available for one month. Select the best answer","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") Dr. Mtweve should not report the case to the DPH at this point in time as it might not be German measles"},{"label":"B","text":") Dr. Mtweve should wait to see if other students develop similar symptoms before reporting the case to the DPH"},{"label":"C","text":") Dr. Mtweve should only report the case to the DPH if the patient has given him permission to do so"},{"label":"D","text":") Dr. Mtweve should report the case to the DPH immediately and tell the patient the case is being reported"},{"label":"E","text":") Dr. Mtweve should consult the World Health Organization’s case definition of German measles to assure his patient meets the case definition before reporting to the DPH"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2d06ea214f5d","stem":"Select the correct statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") The attributable risk is the excess risk of disease in the exposed compared to the non-exposed during a defined period of time"},{"label":"B","text":") The attributable risk is a ratio of the disease risk in the exposed compared to the non-exposed during a defined period of time"},{"label":"C","text":") The attributable risk is a ratio of the disease risk in the non-exposed compared to the exposed during a defined period of time"},{"label":"D","text":") The attributable risk is the prevalence of disease in the exposed minus the prevalence of disease in the non-exposed"},{"label":"E","text":") The attributable risk is the disease risk in a defined group at a specific point in time"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8c322ed680a0cae1eddd73b928d018339f3a84bb"},{"id":"q-c13e5a071409","stem":"Select the correct statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") A study result is more generalizable if it is statistically significant vs. not statistically significant"},{"label":"B","text":") A study result is clinically important if it is statistically significant"},{"label":"C","text":") Failure to blind subjects and investigators is a potential threat to external validity"},{"label":"D","text":") Failure to consider the cost of treatment is a potential threat to internal validity"},{"label":"E","text":") A study result with a p-value less than 0.0000001 might have no clinical importance"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8c322ed680a0cae1eddd73b928d018339f3a84bb"},{"id":"q-1fc89aff9ebc","stem":"Disease X is incurable. It is known that the incidence of Disease X has been constant during the past 30 years. Researchers have determined that the prevalence of Disease X is higher today than it was 15 years ago. Select the answer that best explains this observation","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") Patients with Disease X lived longer 15 years ago compared to today"},{"label":"B","text":") The relative risk of getting Disease X has decreased during the past 15 years"},{"label":"C","text":") The attributable risk of Disease X has increased during the past 15 years"},{"label":"D","text":") The odds ratio of getting Disease X has increased during the past 15 years"},{"label":"E","text":") Patients with Disease X had a shorter life span 15 years ago compared to today"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"476a86fadea40a032b88b7afac7c31336cb1ec74"},{"id":"q-dd0132e3d41d","stem":"The main benefit of a randomized controlled trial (RCT) compared to all other epidemiology study designs is that the RCT","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") is prospective thereby eliminating the need for historical data"},{"label":"B","text":") has better external validity"},{"label":"C","text":") guarantees that confounding bias will not occur"},{"label":"D","text":") tends to equally distribute into the study arms characteristics which may be independent risk factors for the outcome of interest"},{"label":"E","text":") tends to avoid random misclassification of the outcome of interest"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-757dcb3b0c85","stem":"Researchers conduct screening tests on a group of injection drug abusers to detect Hepatitis C. It is known that the prevalence of Hepatitis C in this group is 30%. Researchers then use the same screening test to detect Hepatitis C in a group of college students who are known to have a 1% prevalence of Hepatitis C. Select the best answer. 1.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") The predictive value negative will be higher in the group of college students vs. the group of injection drug abusers."},{"label":"B","text":") The specificity of the Hepatitis C screening test will be higher in the group of college students vs. the group of injection drug abusers."},{"label":"C","text":") The sensitivity of the Hepatitis C screening test will be lower in the group of college students vs. the group of injection drug abusers."},{"label":"D","text":") Both the predictive value positive and the predictive value negative will be higher in the group of injection drug abusers vs. the group of college students."},{"label":"E","text":") The predictive value positive will be higher in the group which has more people tested for Hepatitis C."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d1af6538aca6","stem":"Select the best statement concerning a normal (Gaussian) distribution: 1.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") The variance equals the standard deviation."},{"label":"B","text":") The probability of randomly selecting a variable higher than two standard deviations above the mean is 5%."},{"label":"C","text":") The probability of randomly selecting a variable higher than one standard deviation above the mean is 16%."},{"label":"D","text":") The total area under the curve cannot be calculated unless the mean, median and standard deviation are known."},{"label":"E","text":") The distribution is symmetric about each standard deviation. 2."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7b97b36583da","stem":"Researchers conduct a randomized controlled trial to determine if Medicine A is better than Medicine B in preventing MIs in women with heart disease over the age of 60. The researchers determined that a sample size of 100 would be necessary to have an 80% probability of detecting a true difference of 10% or larger between the two groups. Alpha was set at 0.05. The study results showed that 10% of the women on Medicine A had an MI and 5% of the women on Medicine B had an MI, with P = 0.30. Select the best statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") Given the null hypothesis is true, there is a 30% chance of obtaining a difference of 5% or larger between the two groups."},{"label":"B","text":") Given the null hypothesis is true, there is a 30% chance of obtaining a difference of 10% or larger between the two groups."},{"label":"C","text":") The study’s power is 20% to detect a true difference of 10% or larger."},{"label":"D","text":") The probability that a Type II error occurred is 30%."},{"label":"E","text":") The probability that a Type I error occurred is 30%."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3deca6e6348f","stem":"Assume that height is normally distributed in Tanzanian adults with a mean of 68” and a standard deviation of 2”. Suppose that you randomly select a sample of 100 adults and determine their average height. You repeat this process 35 more times such that you have a total of 36 samples of n = 100. Select the correct statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") The variance in the distribution of sample means will probably be about 4”."},{"label":"B","text":") The standard error of the mean for any one sample will probably be about 18”."},{"label":"C","text":") The distribution of the sample means would probably be wider if there were 50 samples of n = 100 vs. the 36 samples of n = 100."},{"label":"D","text":"The distribution of the sample means must be skewed if the heights are not normally distributed in the Tanzanian population."},{"label":"E","text":"The mean, median and mode of the distribution of the sample means will probably be the same number."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-11479a037407","stem":"Researchers randomly assign 50 subjects to Med A and 50 subjects to Med B. The investigators determine that they need a total sample size of 90 subjects to achieve 80% power to detect a true difference of at least 25% for the outcome. The authors report that 10% of subjects on Med A had the outcome while 12% of subjects on Med B had the outcome. Alpha was set at 0.05 and the resulting p value was 0.15. Select the correct answer","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") Given the alternate hypothesis is true, the study had an 80% chance of detecting a true difference of at least 2%."},{"label":"B","text":") A Type II error occurred."},{"label":"C","text":") If alpha were set at 0.10 and not 0.05, the researchers’ conclusion regarding the study result would remain the same."},{"label":"D","text":") Given the null hypothesis is true, the study had an 80% probability of detecting a true difference of at least 15%."},{"label":"E","text":") Given the null hypothesis is true, the study had a 5% chance of detecting a true difference of at least 15%."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["answer_explanation_mixed_into_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9fad0d2ee93e","stem":"Select the correct answer","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") Study results can be clinically significant even if the results are not statistically significant."},{"label":"B","text":") The p value is a measure of association between the exposure and outcome."},{"label":"C","text":") Calculating a p value is one way of adjusting for a potential con founder."},{"label":"D","text":") The higher the p value, the higher the study’s power."},{"label":"E","text":") The higher the beta, the higher the study’s power."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["answer_explanation_mixed_into_stem","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"c91c4262685c62fad2d21b6633bdcf2204ceab1d"},{"id":"q-073562f8b2c9","stem":"Select the correct statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") As prevalence increases, the predictive value positive increases and the predictive value negative decreases."},{"label":"B","text":") As prevalence decreases, the predictive value negative decreases and the predictive value positive increases."},{"label":"C","text":") As the incidence of disease increases, the predictive value positive must increase and the predictive value negative must decrease."},{"label":"D","text":") There is no relationship between disease prevalence and predictive values."},{"label":"E","text":") A test that is 80% sensitive must have a predictive value positive that is at least 80%."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8c322ed680a0cae1eddd73b928d018339f3a84bb"},{"id":"q-4a8d2069159b","stem":"A sampling method that can be used with either simple random sampling or systemic sampling to ensure adequate representation of certain groups in the sample is called","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Biostatistics","topicOriginal":"Biostatistics","subtopic":"Statistical Inference","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") stratified sampling"},{"label":"B","text":") matching"},{"label":"C","text":") convenience sampling"},{"label":"D","text":") Gaussian sampling"},{"label":"E","text":") adjustment"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Biostatistics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-90dd571c441e","stem":"Researchers conducted a study to determine if there is an association between drinking vs. not drinking caffeine before bed and having vivid nightmares. Both the researchers and the subjects were blinded. After the study was published, it was determined that there were a significant number of misclassifications of the outcome. It is known that the true relative risk for drinking caffeine before bed and having vivid nightmares vs. not drinking caffeine before bed is 1.40. Which of the following statements is most consistent with the information above?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") The study probably reported a relative risk greater than 1.40."},{"label":"B","text":") The study probably reported a relative risk less than 1.40."},{"label":"C","text":") The study probably reported a relative risk of 0."},{"label":"D","text":") The study must have reported a p value less than 0.05."},{"label":"E","text":") The study must have reported a p value greater than 0.05."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["possible_multiple_questions_in_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-63cf856f6509","stem":"Select the correct statement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") Prevalence is a measurement of the burden of disease."},{"label":"B","text":") Prevalence only includes new cases."},{"label":"C","text":") Incidence = (prevalence)(average duration of disease)"},{"label":"D","text":") If prevalence increases, the incidence of disease must also increase."},{"label":"E","text":") Prevalence only includes old cases."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8c322ed680a0cae1eddd73b928d018339f3a84bb"},{"id":"q-638165eadeb4","stem":"Researchers want to compare medicine X vs. medicine Y in preventing strokes in patients at high risk for strokes. The best study to make this determination is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") a randomized controlled trial."},{"label":"B","text":") a prospective cohort study."},{"label":"C","text":") a case control study."},{"label":"D","text":") an intervention case series"},{"label":"E","text":") a cross sectional study."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d93793d564bf","stem":"Researchers want to study Paroxysmal Nocturnal Hemoglobinuria (PNH), a disease with an incidence of about 1 per million per year, which manifests itself during the early adult years. They wonder if there is an association between low birth weight and the later development of PNH. The researchers should conduct a: (Select the best answer.)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") RCT with low birth weight and normal birth weight as the two arms."},{"label":"B","text":") Retrospective cohort study with PNH as one arm and no PNH as the other arm."},{"label":"C","text":") Retrospective cohort study with low birth weight as one arm and normal birth weight as the other arm."},{"label":"D","text":") Case control study with PNH as cases and subjects without PNH as controls"},{"label":"E","text":") Large case series"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2607737641b9","stem":"A RCT of 10,000 adults was conducted to determine if a new anti-platelet medication taken with aspirin is better than aspirin taken alone in subjects with heart disease. The outcome of interest was MI (heart attack). Select the best answer","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") It is possible that confounding by the new anti-platelet medication could occur."},{"label":"B","text":") Comparing one arm taking two medications to another arm taking only one medication will likely lead to interaction by the arm taking the two medications."},{"label":"C","text":") After randomization, the arms will likely be comparable for unknown potential confounders."},{"label":"D","text":") Tablets with the baseline characteristics of both study arms should show both the known and unknown potential confounders."},{"label":"E","text":") Confounding is eliminated by the choice of a RCT."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cefbf705fb20","stem":"Which statement best describes adjustment? Select the best answer.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":") It’s a method to assure there is an equal distribution of potential confounders in study arms."},{"label":"B","text":") It’s used when there is a concern that confounding may lead to an over estimate or an under estimate of the appropriate measure of association."},{"label":"C","text":") It can offset the effects of subjects not adhering to their assigned protocols."},{"label":"D","text":") It’s a study design method to help prevent confounding."},{"label":"E","text":") It’s useful in calculating interaction."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3f21b1ca4623","stem":"Which of the following epidemiologic study designs is best suited for assessing a possible association between an exposure and a rare outcome?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Case Series"},{"label":"B","text":"Prospective Cohort Study"},{"label":"C","text":"Case Control Study"},{"label":"D","text":"Retrospective Cohort Study"},{"label":"E","text":"Cross-Sectional Study"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5caafec46540","stem":"The most important difference between a cohort study and a case control study is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A cohort study is prospective and a case control study is retrospective."},{"label":"B","text":"A case control study can assess multiple outcomes while a cohort study can assess multiple exposures."},{"label":"C","text":"It is easier to assess incidence data in a case control study than in a cohort study."},{"label":"D","text":"At the beginning of the study, a cohort study classifies subjects by exposure status while a case control study classifies subjects by outcome status."},{"label":"E","text":"A prospective cohort study is more prone to selection bias than a case control study."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"5e5cbcd7ec3e66347aa3135a28d04833ab3194ac"},{"id":"q-b01a69dd7c29","stem":"A study reports that regular exercisers have a relative risk of 0.20 compared to non-regular exercisers in the prevention of strokes. The correct interpretation of this relative risk is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Regular exercisers are 20% less likely to develop a stroke vs. non-regular exercisers."},{"label":"B","text":"Regular exercisers have 80% of the risk of developing a stroke vs. non-regular exercisers."},{"label":"C","text":"Non-regular exercisers are 80% less likely to develop a stroke vs. regular exercisers."},{"label":"D","text":"Non-regular exercisers are 20% more likely to develop a stroke vs. regular exercisers."},{"label":"E","text":"Regular exercisers are 80% less likely to develop a stroke vs. non-regular exercisers."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e7259c42b2fd","stem":"Select the correct statement concerning association and causation","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"For an exposure and outcome to be causally related, there must be an association between the exposure and outcome."},{"label":"B","text":"Effect modification (interaction) is a type of non-causal association."},{"label":"C","text":"All of the Bradford Hill criteria for causation must be present for an association between an exposure and outcome to be causally related."},{"label":"D","text":"The relative risk, or odds ratio, must be greater than one for an exposure to be causally related to an outcome."},{"label":"E","text":"The Z score may be used to measure an association between an exposure and outcome."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-149b6b64fbac","stem":"Select the correct statement concerning the selection of controls in a case control study","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Controls should come from a different geographic area than the cases."},{"label":"B","text":"Randomization can help assure comparability of cases and controls."},{"label":"C","text":"Matching can be used to reduce confounding bias."},{"label":"D","text":"It is less important to assure comparability of cases and controls in a case control study than in assuring comparability of study arms in a randomized controlled trial."},{"label":"E","text":"It is best to identify controls with conditions that are related to the outcome in the case control study."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dd9218e25f07","stem":"Select the correct statement concerning the gold standard for a diagnostic test for a particular disease","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"It should be easily performed."},{"label":"B","text":"It should be widely available."},{"label":"C","text":"It should only be used when the prevalence of disease warrants its use."},{"label":"D","text":"It might be the diagnostic test that has the most risk to harm the patient."},{"label":"E","text":"Its predictive value positive should ideally exceed its predictive value negative."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-01d47b430963","stem":"Select the correct statement concerning a chi square test and a two sample t-test","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The p-values resulting from these tests are always interpreted the same way."},{"label":"B","text":"If the variables have been transformed, both tests can be used when assessing categorical data."},{"label":"C","text":"If the categorical data fall into three or more categories, one should use ANOVA rather than the chi square test."},{"label":"D","text":"The two sample t test is more reliable than the chi square test when the sample sizes are small."},{"label":"E","text":"One may use the two sample t test when assessing continuous outcomes in discrete categories if the sample size is large."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1fe4dcd417e3","stem":"Select the false statement concerning a p-value","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Under certain circumstances it can be used to quantify the likelihood that the null hypothesis is false."},{"label":"B","text":"A small difference from a large sample size can have the same p-value as a large difference from a small sample size."},{"label":"C","text":"A p-value less than 0.05 may not be statistically significant."},{"label":"D","text":"A Type I Error could occur with a p-value of 0.02 and alpha set at 0.10."},{"label":"E","text":"A Type II Error might occur if a p-value is 0.15 and alpha was set at 0.10."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c9105db27c45","stem":"The following is a continuous variable","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blood glucose"},{"label":"B","text":"Malaria parasite count"},{"label":"C","text":"Age last birthday"},{"label":"D","text":"Exact age"},{"label":"E","text":"Family size"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-38baf8da6056","stem":"The following method can be used to show the relationship between two variables","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histogram"},{"label":"B","text":"Pie chart"},{"label":"C","text":"Scatter diagram"},{"label":"D","text":"Bar chart"},{"label":"E","text":"Line graph"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"12dbcb39ff11973300013776300cf3abd47d2e9f"},{"id":"q-df7ff963b1e4","stem":"Descriptive statistics include","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Assessing measure of variation"},{"label":"B","text":"Comparing sample means between studies"},{"label":"C","text":"Describing sample estimates"},{"label":"D","text":"Simple analysis of data"},{"label":"E","text":"Drawing graphs and charts"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dd316ad55fb0","stem":"When the distribution is skewed to the right","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The median is greater than the mean"},{"label":"B","text":"The distribution is unimodal"},{"label":"C","text":"The tail of the left is shorter than the tail on the right"},{"label":"D","text":"The standard deviation is less than the variance"},{"label":"E","text":"The majority of the observations are less than the mean"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"f7608bbd7aa6a014171cb4ab9fab5160eedcc42b"},{"id":"q-763b5e0793e2","stem":"The following variable has the interval scale of measurement","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"height"},{"label":"B","text":"presence or absence of asthma"},{"label":"C","text":"temperature measurement"},{"label":"D","text":"age"},{"label":"E","text":"the size of your shoes"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1b2f26707667","stem":"The mean of a large sample","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"is always greater than the median"},{"label":"B","text":"is calculated from the formula Σx/n"},{"label":"C","text":"is from an approximately Normal distribution"},{"label":"D","text":"increases as the sample increases"},{"label":"E","text":"is always greater than the standard deviation"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-073a12023092","stem":"The standard error of the mean of a sample","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"measure the variability of the observations"},{"label":"B","text":"is the accuracy with which each observation is measured"},{"label":"C","text":"is a measure of how far the sample mean is likely to be from the population mean"},{"label":"D","text":"is proportion to the number of observations"},{"label":"E","text":"is greater than the estimated standard deviation of the population"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ebd1326d1376","stem":"When comparing the means of two large samples using the Standard Normal Deviate test","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the null hypothesis is that the sample means are equal"},{"label":"B","text":"the null hypothesis is that the means are not significantly different"},{"label":"C","text":"standard error of the difference is the sum of the standard errors of the means"},{"label":"D","text":"the standard errors of the means must be equal"},{"label":"E","text":"the test statistics is the ratio of the difference to its standard error"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1e4f1c49896e","stem":"The paired t-test is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"impractical for large samples"},{"label":"B","text":"useful for the analysis of qualitative data"},{"label":"C","text":"suitable for very small samples"},{"label":"D","text":"used for independent samples"},{"label":"E","text":"based on the normal distribution"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ab304df7a787","stem":"The P-value","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"is the probability that the null hypothesis is false"},{"label":"B","text":"is large for studies with small sample sizes"},{"label":"C","text":"ranges between -1 and +1"},{"label":"D","text":"is similar to Type I Error"},{"label":"E","text":"is similar to Type II Error"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["short_or_incomplete_stem"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["short_or_incomplete_stem","short_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-91abf6990ac2","stem":"The following condition must be met for a valid t-test for two sample means","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the number of observations must be the same in the two groups"},{"label":"B","text":"the standard deviations must be approximately the same in the two groups"},{"label":"C","text":"the means must be approximately equal in the two groups"},{"label":"D","text":"the observations must be from approximately Normal distributions"},{"label":"E","text":"The sample must be small"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-08fcda361552","stem":"Data from an urban area of Tanzania show that the mean age of terminating breastfeeding is 6 months and the median is 3 months. From these facts we conclude that","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"approximately 50% of the children breastfeed for more than 6 months"},{"label":"B","text":"the mode duration of breastfeeding is probably 9 months"},{"label":"C","text":"the mode duration of breastfeeding is probably is less than 6 months"},{"label":"D","text":"the distribution of breastfeeding is not symmetric"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-572681f88591","stem":"The sample size required for a study to estimate a mean","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"depends on the widths of the confidence interval which we want"},{"label":"B","text":"depends on the variability of the quantity being studied"},{"label":"C","text":"depends on the power we wish to have"},{"label":"D","text":"depends on the anticipated value of the mean"},{"label":"E","text":"depends on the anticipated value of the standard deviation"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-16502473ca92","stem":"We can use paired t-test for the following comparisons","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"compare costs: a sample of patients from KCMC hospital and another from Muhimbili hospital"},{"label":"B","text":"Spending between boys and girls. Select 100 brother-sister pairs"},{"label":"C","text":"Subjects are asked about height. They are then measured, test for the differences"},{"label":"D","text":"Study effect of health education. Knowledge before and after workshop"},{"label":"E","text":"Durability (as measured number of days) of two types of socks. People wear one type of sock on one foot and another type on other leg"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e03bfdbd52d8","stem":"The x2 test is used for","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"comparison between two continuous variables"},{"label":"B","text":"comparison between one continuous variable and one categorical variable"},{"label":"C","text":"comparison between two categorical variables"},{"label":"D","text":"comparison between more than one categorical variable"},{"label":"E","text":"comparison between column and row variables SECTION II: TRUE OR FALSE QUESTIONS (Total 25 marks) Indicate which of the following statements is True or False by indicating T (for true) or F (for false) in the answer book. ABOUT EPIDEMIOLOGICAL PRINCIPLES"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2372e878f16c","stem":"The following four tables are SPSS outputs looking at some factors associated with obesity. The independent factors include sex (male and female) and age (in years). (Total 15 marks). Table 1. Distribution of the study participants by sex Sex n (%) Male 2049(43.6) Female 2650(56.4) Total 4699 (100) Table 2. Age descriptive measure N minimum maximum mean std deviation Age 4699 30 68 46.04 8.50 Valid 4699 Table 3. Obesity in study population Obese n (%) Yes 4089(87.2) No 4699(12.8) Total 4699 (100) Table 4. Comparison of mean age among obese and non-obese study participants | |t-test for Equality of Means | | | | |Sig. |Mean difference |Std. Error | | | | | |(2-tailed) | |difference |95% CI | | |t |df | | | | | | | | | | | |Lower |Upper | |AGE |6.870 |1688 |0.000 |2.54 |0.369 |1.814 |3.262 |","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Describe briefly what Tables 1, 2 and 3 show ( 6 marks)"},{"label":"B","text":"State the null and alternative hypotheses being tested in table 4 ( 2 marks)"},{"label":"C","text":"Explain, in Table 4, what does the 95% confidence interval mean? (5 marks) ABOUT SCREENING"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1bfbd554caf0","stem":"A study was conducted among former mine workers to assess the misclassification probabilities of a Chest X-Ray in the diagnosis of silicosis (a fibrotic chronic lung disease that is caused by prolonged occupational inhalation of silica dust). Chest X-Rays taken one month before the death of the subject were used and histological examination of the lung tissue (obtained at post mortem irrespective of the cause of death) was undertaken. Histology results were considered to be the reference or gold standard. The following results were obtained (15 marks). | | |Histology Test Results | | | | | |Total | | | |Positive |Negative | | |Chest X-Ray results |Positive |775 | |1,050 | | |Negative | |1,300 | | |Total |950 |1,550 |2,500 | (i) Complete the missing figures in the remaining cells (3 marks) (ii) Calculate the sensitivity of Chest X-Ray (2 marks) (iii) Calculate the specificity of Chest X-Ray (2 marks) (iv) Calculate the predictive value of a positive chest X-Ray (2 marks) (v) Interpret your results (4 marks) (vi) Which among the following would be affected by the prevalence of the disease (silicosis) in the population being studied?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-015df5887aa3","stem":"The following data were gathered from the Framingham Heart Study, an ongoing study in the town of Framingham, Massachusetts. In 1972, 1800 men were examined and it was determined that 500 had high blood pressure (HBP). The men were then followed for the next 18 years to determine how many developed cardiovascular disease (CVD) during that time period. One hundred and sixty men were diagnosed with cardiovascular disease; of these 160, ninety were among the 500 that were initially diagnosed with high blood pressure as well. [10 marks]","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"What kind of study is this? (2)"},{"label":"B","text":"Use a table provided below to construct a 2x2 table for these data and enter the relevant labels and data in the appropriate cells: (2) | |High BP |No high BP |Total | |CVD |90 | |160 | |No CVD | | | | |Total |500 | |1,800 |"},{"label":"C","text":"Using the above information, (i) Name the appropriate measure of association (2) (ii) Calculate the appropriate measure of association and (2 marks) (iii) Interpret the results what it means: (2 marks) END OF EXAM From the Department: Best of luck with your medical career"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-24f2df7e7ed9","stem":"drawn from two different populations, which one of the following conditions should be satisfied?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-50aa6df12c96","stem":"used to test the hypothesis?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Biostatistics","topicOriginal":"Biostatistics","subtopic":"Statistical Inference","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Biostatistics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cef6393a8c2f","stem":"c) Explain, in Table 4, what does the 95% confidence interval mean? (5 marks)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Biostatistics and Research","topic":"Biostatistics","topicOriginal":"Biostatistics","subtopic":"Statistical Inference","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Biostatistics and Research","displayTopic":"Biostatistics","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d071717729ff","stem":"The following table shows data from an epidemiological study. What type of study was this most likely to be?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"case-control"},{"label":"B","text":"cohort"},{"label":"C","text":"randomised controlled trial"},{"label":"D","text":"cross-sectional"},{"label":"E","text":"descriptive"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e1141aa6f432","stem":"A randomized, placebo-controlled trial was conducted in Indonesia to study the effects of Vitamin A in preventing deaths among children with measles. The investigators reported a relative risk of 0.60 for the intervention versus control group. This means that To assess the association between Kawasaki syndrome (KS) and carpet shampoo, investigators conducted a case-control study with 100 cases (children with KS) and 100 controls (children without KS). Among the children with KS, 50 had a history of recent exposure to carpet shampoo. Among the controls, the number with a recent history of exposure to carpet shampoo was 25. For this study, the odds ratio was: What type of case-control study within this cohort would be ideal to study multiple outcomes, and why is the alternative case-control design not recommended?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"children receiving vitamin a were 40% less likely to die from measles than children receiving placebo"},{"label":"B","text":"the chance of dying from measles in the placebo group was 60%"},{"label":"C","text":"the chance of the null hypothesis being true is 60%"},{"label":"D","text":"60% of the children who died received vitamin a; the other 40% received placebo"},{"label":"E","text":"none of the above statements are true"},{"label":"A","text":"1.0"},{"label":"B","text":"2.0"},{"label":"C","text":"3.0"},{"label":"D","text":"cannot be calculated from the information given"},{"label":"B","text":"Basic design in analytical epidemiology: A case-control is conducted within a well-defined cohort. The reason for this is that expensive additional data collection is needed, and the budget is not sufficient to obtain this data from all cohort participants."}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-816c19a37d4a","stem":"What is the fraction of cases with the disease among the exposed that is attributable to the exposure? Answer: a","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-98425b6088a9","stem":"What would have been the relative risk for COPD in this study if the heavy smokers were used as the reference category? Answer: a","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7e9a872b74b9","stem":"What is the diagnostic value of a positive test in this situation? Answer: a","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-caa0d6f99b06","stem":"What can be concluded from the results of this study?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0dd715718430","stem":"An epidemiologist performed a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled trial to examine the effect of fish oil supplementation on memory complaints. In the group that received fish oil, 19% of the participants forgot to take their daily supplements during the study whereas this was only 3% in the placebo group. Is this a problem? Answer: b","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4c019cc4f41f","stem":"Which of the following measures does not increase the internal validity of the study? Answer: d","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4027bc62e5ba","stem":"Is this conclusion justified? Answer: c","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":