R = 9.85 (95% CI = 0.89 - 36.48)"},{"label":"C","text":"OR = 1.89 (95% CI = 1.81 - 1.95)"},{"label":"D","text":"OR = 1.91 (95% CI = 1.02 - 2.89)"},{"label":"E","text":"None"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-408bb6a7de0c","stem":"If we wanted to obtain a measure of the incidence of breast cancer among women in Kilimanjaro during 2007, what breast cancer cases would we count in the numerator?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"All cases of breast cancer among women in Kilimanjaro in 2007"},{"label":"B","text":"Only newly diagnosed cases of breast cancer among women in Kilimanjaro in 2007?"},{"label":"C","text":"All women in kilimanjaro region"},{"label":"D","text":"All women at reproductive age only at Kilimanjaro"},{"label":"E","text":"Only women susceptible to breast cancer"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3fedcfe3901436155346573e1cd6b30bfb5a7734"},{"id":"q-430e0988356e","stem":"Assuming that we begin to measure incidence on January 1, 2007, which Kilimanjaro women would be counted for the denominator of the incidence measure?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"All women in Kilimanjaro in2007."},{"label":"B","text":"Only women in Kilimanjaro without breast cancer on January 1, 2007."},{"label":"C","text":"All women in Kilimanjaro with and without breast cancer"},{"label":"D","text":"A and B are most correct"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"aed85da9453553e20224b695c5af6e02a9345f08"},{"id":"q-c60897508cb0","stem":"To measure the prevalence of breast cancer among women in Kilimanjaro for the year 2007, what breast cancer cases would we count in the numerator?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"All breast cancer cases reported to the Kilimanjaro Cancer Registry in 2007"},{"label":"B","text":"All breast cancer cases ever reported to the Kilimanjaro Cancer Registry?"},{"label":"C","text":"All surviving breast cancer cases ever reported to the Kilimanjaro Cancer Registry?"},{"label":"D","text":"A and B are correct"},{"label":"E","text":"All are correct"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3e502c12c23fc35750be0eb2367b9e9502b33d6f"},{"id":"q-6fe8fd42bd41","stem":"A hypothetical study of the effect of alcohol intake on the risk of gallstones in Tanzanians aged 20-74 was conducted. Ultrasonography of the gallbladder of 2200 study subjects identified 152 subjects with gallstones, 42 of whom listed their alcohol consumption level as \"high\". Of the men without gallstones, 220 listed their alcohol intake level as \"high\". Interviews were conducted at the same time as the examinations to determine the exposure level of study participants. Which measure of association is most appropriate in this cross-sectional design?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prevalence ratio"},{"label":"B","text":"Prevalence odds ratio"},{"label":"C","text":"Cumulative incidence"},{"label":"D","text":"None of the above"},{"label":"E","text":"A and B are correct"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"ef825357d89e67e0fa65fde357291dc6a690f03c"},{"id":"q-69e33ea1435f","stem":". What happens to the prevalence of a disease in a population when the average duration of the disease increases?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"it increases"},{"label":"B","text":"it decreases"},{"label":"C","text":"it stays the same"},{"label":"D","text":"it depends with disease"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"23b63ba51736330a257a0d30fbfaf8b6b83495bf","manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-1c72c55cef5a","stem":"What can happen to the size of an open cohort over time?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"May shrink/decrease"},{"label":"B","text":"May grow"},{"label":"C","text":"May remain constant"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d834e661406b1aab230066218cb0f27f48c9b421"},{"id":"q-0a3f48b4f08c","stem":"What types of criteria may be used to construct a case definition?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Clinical"},{"label":"B","text":"Personal characteristics"},{"label":"C","text":"Region and time of occurrence"},{"label":"D","text":"All of the above"},{"label":"E","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0e3322e59975","stem":"Which the following actions are most likely to preserve the benefit of initial randomization in a clinical trial comparing different weight loss programs?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blinding of the clinicians who evaluate weight loss"},{"label":"B","text":"The use of a comparison group that receives only a pharmacologically inert substance"},{"label":"C","text":"Measurement of patient satisfaction with treatment rather than actual reduction in weight"},{"label":"D","text":"Increasing the number of patients studied"},{"label":"E","text":"Restricting the study population to patients with mild obesity"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9ab40e52193f","stem":"We wish to investigate if a new medicine is effective in reducing the length and severity of the flu. We take the next 20 patients that come to the walk-in clinic complaining of flu and, after a medical examination to verify that the patients do have the flu, we give them the new medicine and tell them about the new drug we are giving them. One week later, the patients are contacted and 15 patients state the new remedy was helpful in reducing the severity and length of the illness. Which of the following is NOT CORRECT?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a65c19486e2b","stem":"Which of the following is CORRECT?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"We do not need to randomize if our sample size is sufficiently large."},{"label":"B","text":"A large sample size always ensures that our sample is representative of the population."},{"label":"C","text":"If all other things are equal, we need a larger sample size for a larger population."},{"label":"D","text":"In a properly chosen sample, an estimate will be less variable with a large sample size and hence more precise."},{"label":"E","text":"In random samples, the randomization ensures that we get precise and accurate estimates."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"dd94cde0d572d59d908db1abc4ef5afbb72d56d7"},{"id":"q-60d27d3437f0","stem":"When disease is considered from epidemiological perspective, the focus is on its proximate or immediate cause of disease (pathogen, diet, home environment) but biomedical approach also considering underlying population causes of disease (cultural, economic and social). ...........","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-8e6140e4d93c","stem":"When only study subjects are unaware of their treatment status but investigator and analyst are aware of the treatment status, the trial is called triple –blinded trial .........","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-c4a11202eefa","stem":"Read the sentence below and respond either YES or NO and defend your choice of answer (Total 5 marks) Sentence: Suppose that in a case-control study using incident cases of colon cancer you found that 80% of the cases were married. Does this demonstrate that being married increases the risk of developing colon cancer?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3e4862f06d19","stem":"A hypothetical study of the effect of alcohol intake on the risk of gallstones in Tanzanian aged 20-74 was conducted. Ultrasonography of the gallbladder of 2200 study subjects identified 152 subjects with gallstones, 42 of whom listed their alcohol consumption level as \"high\". Of the men without gallstones, 220 listed their alcohol intake level as \"high\". Interviews were conducted at the same time as the examinations to determine the exposure level of study participants. Which measure of association is most appropriate in the cross-sectional design?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Prevalence ratio"},{"label":"B","text":"Prevalence odds ratio"},{"label":"C","text":"Cumulative incidence"},{"label":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-10f53ef856a0","stem":"Researchers want to assess if there is an association between cigarette smoking and strokes. Of the following, which is the best study design to assess for this association?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Case series"},{"label":"B","text":"Cross-Sectional study"},{"label":"C","text":"Prospective cohort study"},{"label":"D","text":"Randomized controlled trial"},{"label":"E","text":"Intervention study"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3c749d60be5914fcf61ee9d775b6c28252a57820"},{"id":"q-6440bcbca75f","stem":"A study in which children are randomly assigned to receive either a newly formulated vaccine or the currently available vaccine, and are followed to monitor for side effects and effectiveness of each vaccine, is an example of which type of study?","type":"Calculation","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Experimental"},{"label":"B","text":"Observational"},{"label":"C","text":"Cohort"},{"label":"D","text":"Case-control"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9071242fdef8","stem":"The Moshi Women’s Health Study, in which researchers enrolled 2654 women in 2002 and collected exposure and lifestyle information to assess the relationship between these factors and subsequent occurrence of cancer, is an example of which type of study?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Experimental"},{"label":"B","text":"Clinical trial"},{"label":"C","text":"Cohort"},{"label":"D","text":"Case-control"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f8e190b017bd","stem":"Pediatric department at KCMC conducted a study to compare DPT vaccine history among 1200 children with pervasive developmental disorder (autism) and 2400 children without such disorder. They found no association. This is an example of which type of study?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Experimental"},{"label":"B","text":"Clinical trial"},{"label":"C","text":"Cohort"},{"label":"D","text":"Case-control"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d3fd2753160a","stem":"A distribution in which the median is greater than the mean is described as being: What is the most likely shape of distribution of observations in the following studies in question 58 & 59?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Skewed towards the left"},{"label":"B","text":"Skewed toward the right"},{"label":"C","text":"Only slightly skewed"},{"label":"D","text":"Moderately skewed"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d6cc3b03ed2c","stem":"Which of the following descriptive statistics is least affected by outliers?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mean"},{"label":"B","text":"Median"},{"label":"C","text":"Range"},{"label":"D","text":"Standard deviation"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"reasoned_without_reference","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"4cffb4623e1cd9db855cd2d4ba9de5a6989499b8","manualEdits":{}},{"id":"q-b94b96e3fe69","stem":"Which of the following is not a measure of variability?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Correlation coefficient"},{"label":"B","text":"Variance"},{"label":"C","text":"Standard deviation"},{"label":"D","text":"Range"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"7d1fa57c0c7c742661a8dd2acf713c34bb06e445"},{"id":"q-c4872523c4bb","stem":"2marks @ = 10 marks","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"to compare BMI between men and women"},{"label":"B","text":"Was 2.2 with 95% CI: 0.1 to 4.3"},{"label":"C","text":"Women has higher BMI by 2.2 than men"},{"label":"D","text":"Mean"},{"label":"E","text":"Probably because the distribution of the weight and height was normally distributed"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-decba69af48e","stem":"clinical trials","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Advantages of cross-sectional study( any 2 of these @ 1.5 marks = 3pts) i) easy and fast to conduct ii) Cheap iii) Rapid assessment of the health situation iv) Used for health planning v) Can provide prevalence"},{"label":"C","text":"Disadvantages of cross-sectional study (any 2 of these @ 1.5 marks = 3pts) i) Does not provide temporal association between cause and outcome ii) Because it is conducted at one point in time, can not predict trends over time iii) Can not estimate incidence of disease"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag","repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3c8b89c5631ef2b52584b3f60d3570314bc6784b"},{"id":"q-211540ea69f2","stem":"Total 10 marks","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Case-control ( 1 mark)"},{"label":"B","text":"There are cases and controls among the participants. Both cases and controls were either exposed or not exposed to the risk factor. Only cases have outcome (disease) of interest (total 3marks)."},{"label":"C","text":"Advantages and disadvantages of case-control study Advantages (any 2 of these pts @ 1.5 marks) - relative quick, cheap and easy to perform - suitable for rare disease - used to study multiple exposures/risk factors - no loss of follow-up Disadvantages (any 2 of these pts @ 1.5 marks) - recall bias - not suitable for disease with rare exposure - If onset of disease precede exposure to risk, causation can not be inferred."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["short_or_incomplete_stem"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["short_or_incomplete_stem","short_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1d755c80e0a2","stem":"2@ x 10 = total 20 marks","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Retrospective study: data on disease status or exposure status are collected after the events have occurred i.e. use pre-existing data on exposure and outcomes (as opposed to prospective data collection). Prospective study/incidence study/longitudinal or follow-up study: identify the participants and then follow them up over time until the end-point of interest has been reached or the time limit for the study has been reached (study in which data are collected in an going way during the study)."},{"label":"B","text":"Population: A group of individuals living in a particular area or all cases that meet pre-set criteria and sample: A subset of units in the population or a group of subjects selected from a population."},{"label":"C","text":"Confounder: is a variable or a factor that is associated with both the exposure and outcome, but is not in a causal pathway between them. i.e. a confounder is also a risk factor for the outcome in its own right, and bias: any error that results in a systematic deviation from the estimation of the association between exposure and outcome or an explanation for the findings of a study( information/measurement bias and or selection bias)"},{"label":"D","text":"Research proposal: A project proposal is written, to make an offer and to try to convince a supervisor or a future customer to accept it (it details how research will be undertaken i.e. contains; research title, introduction, objectives and methodology). and research report: an outline how the research was conducted, it also gives findings from the research undertaken (i.e. contains; research title, introduction, objectives and methodology)"},{"label":"E","text":"Informed consent: is a legal condition whereby a person can be said to have given consent based upon an appreciation and understanding of the facts and implications of an action. The individual needs to be in possession of relevant facts and also of his or her reasoning abilities, such as not being mentally retarded or mentally ill and without an impairment of judgment at the time of consenting. and written consent: Written consent should be gained wherever possible to ensure that information is being collected and provided in a consistent and uniform way. It also acts as a guarantee should any form of dispute arise. it requires an individual to sign on a sheet of paper as an agreement to participate in a study."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f5e572a43f2b","stem":"Total 10 marks i) a) Numerical discrete (quantitative)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"B","text":"Categorical binary"},{"label":"C","text":"Categorical binary ii) a) Sex: 8 or 2 responses ( male and female)"},{"label":"B","text":"Smoker: 8 or 2 responses (yes and no) iii) Proportion of male and female who smokes Male: 4/8 = 0.5 or 50% Female: 0/8 = 0"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8977543d964c","stem":"List at least 9 areas in which public health professionals utilize epidemiology or epidemiological methods (total 5 marks) - Describe the burden of disease/ describe the health status of the population (ecological, cross sectional studies, cohort) - Assess causation or risk factors for disease (field/ analytical) - Outbreak investigation (descriptive and case-control design) - Evaluation of programs, treatment or policies (cohort, RCT, cross sectional) - Surveillance of disease (trends e.g. cross sectional studies repeated) - Describe or document natural history of disease (cohort design) - Interpreting public health literature - Policy formation - Personal decisions (exercise vs. not to do so; eat vegetables vs. not ) - Prediction/ modelling About Epidemiologic study designs","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cdc29cc8ac8e","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a measure of central tendency?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Geometric mean"},{"label":"B","text":"Median"},{"label":"C","text":"Mode"},{"label":"D","text":"Arithmetic mean"},{"label":"E","text":"All these are measures of central tendency"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-334408204841","stem":"How does the computation of a sample variance differ from computation of a population variance?","type":"Calculation","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"N is replaced by n-1"},{"label":"B","text":"N is replaced by n"},{"label":"C","text":"( is replaced by X2"},{"label":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8b39c132cb91","stem":"In a positively skewed distribution, which is correct? What is the most likely shape of distribution of observations in the following studies in question 13-16?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":". Median is greater than mean"},{"label":"B","text":". Median and mean are equal"},{"label":"C","text":". Mean is greater than the median"},{"label":"D","text":"Mean and median are slightly different"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-12f0e55cf3a1","stem":"When the distribution is skewed to the right","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The median is greater than the mean"},{"label":"B","text":"The distribution is unimodal"},{"label":"C","text":"The tail of the left is shorter than the tail on the right"},{"label":"D","text":"The majority of the observations are less than the mean"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"f7608bbd7aa6a014171cb4ab9fab5160eedcc42b"},{"id":"q-7ccd1ae90afb","stem":"The sex, weight, temperature and stages of disease of the patients are examples of which level of data measurement?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nominal, ordinal, interval and ratio respectively"},{"label":"B","text":"Nominal, ratio, interval and ordinal respectively"},{"label":"C","text":"Nominal, ratio, ordinal and interval respectively"},{"label":"D","text":"Nominal, ratio, ordinal and interval respectively"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["duplicate_option_text"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_text","nonunique_option_text","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-96f00b88b5a8","stem":"What are the two types of research methods?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Categorical and numerical."},{"label":"B","text":"Analytical and numerical."},{"label":"C","text":"Interview and questionnaire."},{"label":"D","text":"Quantitative and qualitative."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3b3709e2d647a1cab9b205e20fa24938daf90063"},{"id":"q-f31ce1b6aa63","stem":"Which of the following statement is NOT correct?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Some data sets do not have means"},{"label":"B","text":"Calculation of a mean is affected by extreme data values"},{"label":"C","text":"A weighted mean should be used when it is necessary to take the importance of each value into account"},{"label":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"5004361855bac1b0992212fbf015de4cdf411282"},{"id":"q-83b67af53e92","stem":"Which of the following is the first step in calculating the median of a data set?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Average the middle two values of the data set."},{"label":"B","text":"Arrange the data in ascending or descending order"},{"label":"C","text":"Determine the relative weights of the data values in terms of importance"},{"label":"D","text":"None of these"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d2737ce93d24","stem":"Researcher wants to assess if there is an association between cigarette smoking and strokes. Of the following, which is the best study design to assess for this association?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Case series"},{"label":"B","text":"Cross-sectional study"},{"label":"C","text":"Prospective cohort study"},{"label":"D","text":"Randomized controlled trial"},{"label":"E","text":"Intervention study"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-39dd70125528","stem":"The following data shows duration of hospitalization for 24 children admitted last month to hospital with diarrhoea. (10 points) |ID number |No of days hospitalized |ID number |No of days hospitalized | |Child 1 |06 |Child 13 |10 | |Child 2 |07 |Child 14 |18 | |Child 3 |10 |Child 15 |14 | |Child 4 |08 |Child 16 |12 | |Child 5 |11 |Child 17 |11 | |Child 6 |08 |Child 18 |10 | |Child 7 |04 |Child 19 |10 | |Child 8 |17 |Child 20 |15 | |Child 9 |15 |Child 21 |05 | |Child 10 |14 |Child 22 |12 | |Child 11 |08 |Child 23 |06 | |Child 12 |11 |Child 24 |11 | 3 A. Which scale of measurements will you use for the following variables?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"What was the mean duration of hospitalization? (2) Efx/n = 253/24 = 10.54"},{"label":"B","text":"Calculate median (2) Median = (n + 1)/2...Its nth number = 24+1/2 = 12.5th number ( (10+11)/2 = 10.5"},{"label":"C","text":"What is the standard deviation? (3) SD = √( (X – Mean)2/n-1 = √Variance = √ 13.65 = 3.695"},{"label":"D","text":"Calculate range (1) Range = 14 Or age ranges from 4 years to 18 years"},{"label":"E","text":"What is the likely shape of distribution when you consider mean and median? (1) It is normally distributed because mean and median are nearly equal"},{"label":"D","text":"Is range or standard deviation a better measure of the distribution in this case (1) Standard deviation is a better measure. It is used with the mean, and looking at SD the variability around the mean is not big. SD has an advantage of using all the observations Range: mostly used with median/ordinal variables. Uses only 2 observations and distorted by outliers. For each variable, give the level of measurement and measure of central tendency (MCT) you will use to summarize the data (5 points)"},{"label":"A","text":"Blood haemoglobin level Scale of measurement: ratio/continuous numerical scale MCT: mean"},{"label":"B","text":"Outcome of malaria treatment Scale of measurement: nominal scale MCT: mode"},{"label":"C","text":"Stage of cancer of the stomach Scale of measurement: ordinal scale MCT: median"},{"label":"D","text":"Severity of dysponea Scale of measurement: ordinal scale MCT: median"},{"label":"E","text":"Number of hospital visits Scale of measurement: discrete numerical scale MCT: mean 3 B. Study the following figure and answer the questions that follow (5 points) [pic] Figure 1. Prevalence of HIV by marital status among women in Moshi urban"},{"label":"A","text":"What was the dependent variable? (1) HIV status"},{"label":"B","text":". What was the independent variable? (1) Marital status"},{"label":"C","text":"Which scale of measurement was used for the outcome variable? (1) Nominal scale of measurement"},{"label":"D","text":"Which scale of measurement was used for the predictor variable? (1) Nominal scale of measurement"},{"label":"A","text":"What measure of central tendency and dispersion will you use to summarize the data and why? (1) Mode and variation ratio. The data measured fit into categories and are not numerical"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-3125533d22e0","stem":"Which of the following is NOT correct about the principles of causal inference?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"b66d916a63879d393af3003dbf0188ce24570be1"},{"id":"q-b207e5a84751","stem":"Which one of the following statements is FALSE?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"9b57d62be4e747c314e4219d8143815571529b28"},{"id":"q-e1b5c2c028d4","stem":"In general, which of the following statements is FALSE?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a502a4fa8258099fe45ae0d701da9c225be90b39"},{"id":"q-e2c50880742a","stem":"For the following histogram, what is the proper ordering of the mean, median, and mode?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bad88c59310b","stem":"Which of the following statements is NOT true?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"7ce3019b6ded553e3d04663fba4a7c455dafac68"},{"id":"q-396a8d7246d0","stem":"We wish to investigate if a new medicine is effective in reducing the length and severity of the flu. We take the next 20 patients that come to the walk-in clinic complaining of flu and, after a medical exam to verify that the patients do have the flu, we give them the new medicine and tell them about the new drug we are giving them. One week later, the patients are contacted and 15 patients state the new remedy was helpful in reducing the severity and length of the illness. Which of the following is NOT CORRECT?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8d6a1c5dfcc0a6e4ab553f9ab636b840bf44561f"},{"id":"q-19da05fcbf9a","stem":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"ddd53843a7c3208057acb162a2c5585b42e5b16c"},{"id":"q-dd55eede3f18","stem":"What is a statistical inference?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"232a0dfac95293f2443be08d2c5a013ee8f0342c"},{"id":"q-fc8fc2de21ad","stem":"Name the type of variable indicated in this table...Both Qualitative and Quantitative data.........","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Epidemiolog","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-9b02e2ea2ac4","stem":"When disease is considered from epidemiological perspective, the focus is on its proximate or immediate cause of disease (pathogen, diet, home environment) but biomedical approach also considering underlying population causes of disease (cultural, economic and social). ( F)","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Community Health and Public Health","topic":"Epidemiology","topicOriginal":"Epidemiology","subtopic":"Measures and Study Designs","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Community Health and Public Health","displayTopic":"Epidemiology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8ea6c5483353","stem":"Good 2. Capital 3. Domestic Market Ideas from different scholars on the meaning of development Walter Rodney (1972) development in human society is a many-sided process. At the level of the individual it implies increased capacity (a) Meaning of Development from different scholars i. Jacob Singer explained that in the political science particularly in the international relations policies and politics we can see that there are units and levels of analysis. Development as a unit of analysis can be analyzed into individual level, national level, regional level and global level. ii. Walter Rodney (1972) on his book \"How Europe underdeveloped Africa\". Page 9 What is Development?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Development Studies","topic":"Social Development","topicOriginal":"Social Development","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Development Studies","displayTopic":"Social Development","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-65d9d2aa277c","stem":"3 Ideas from different scholars on the meaning of development Walter Rodney (1972) development in human society is a many-sided process. At the level of the individual it implies increased capacity (a) Meaning of Development from different scholars i. Jacob Singer explained that in the political science particularly in the international relations policies and politics we can see that there are units and levels of analysis. Development as a unit of analysis can be analyzed into individual level, national level, regional level and global level. ii. Walter Rodney (1972) on his book \"How Europe underdeveloped Africa\". Page 9 What is Development?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Development Studies","topic":"Social Development","topicOriginal":"Social Development","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Development Studies","displayTopic":"Social Development","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c938f14774a4","stem":"Communalism: • This is the highest stage of social development. • There was no exploitation of man by man. • The level of investments and production were primarily determined by national plans. CRITICISMS/LIMITATIONS OF THE THEORY: • Bourgeoisie criticizes Marx that his arguments of class struggle and class conflict are too ideological which cannot be successfully implemented in the society. • That the society can never develop without exploitation that is without classes. • Marx believed on working class as an instrument of leading real revolution in the society, but this is not true because real revolution should come in collaboration between workers and peasants. Workers alone can never bring real revolution in the society. (May be he was right at that time: but a theory has to hold true for all times!). • Marx believed on class conflict as important aspect for development but he is contradicting himself because in socialism and communalism modes there is no class conflict so the question comes: how could socialism and communalism emerge as the highest stage of human development while there is no any exploitation of man by many?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Development Studies","topic":"Social Development","topicOriginal":"Social Development","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Development Studies","displayTopic":"Social Development","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-9c3cbec103df","stem":"What is New About Globalization?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Development Studies","topic":"Social Development","topicOriginal":"Social Development","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Development Studies","displayTopic":"Social Development","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-00b32a0db21b","stem":"When evaluating the quality of life in a country, numerous statistics can give an insightful glimpse into the daily life of any population. The World Factbook has provided numerous points mentioned here that will give a clear view of the problems that the Tanzanian population currently faces. Malaria and HIV are primary causes of death for children and adults respectively in Tanzania. The HIV prevalence rate here is at 4.5% - the 13th highest in the world.","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Development Studies","topic":"Social Development","topicOriginal":"Social Development","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Development Studies","displayTopic":"Social Development","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4ed087380f6e","stem":"which has a population of 4.365 million. The capitol of the","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Development Studies","topic":"Social Development","topicOriginal":"Social Development","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Development Studies","displayTopic":"Social Development","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c9df503b0603","stem":"When evaluating the quality of life in a country, numerous","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Development Studies","topic":"Social Development","topicOriginal":"Social Development","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Development Studies","displayTopic":"Social Development","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d9403f