","text":"Pin point pupils T"},{"label":"C","text":"A withdrawal response following administration of naloxone T"},{"label":"D","text":"Convulsion on withdrawal T"},{"label":"E","text":"Cirrhosis of the liver F"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a3483fc0c64c2f5e8010442a51371c972aa63953"},{"id":"q-c2c8e3db08c3","stem":"Which of the following occur as a complication of morphine treatment","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"CNS Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Addiction F"},{"label":"B","text":"Antihistaminic effect F"},{"label":"C","text":"A rise in systemic arterial blood pressure F"},{"label":"D","text":"Constriction of smooth muscle sphincters T"},{"label":"E","text":"Stimulation of respiratory centre F"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"1e1d15eadba640c44d54c735f9530c17def39d42"},{"id":"q-39235cc13c43","stem":"heroine (Diamorphine)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"CNS Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Has greater efficacy with less adverse effects than does morphine F"},{"label":"B","text":"Has only one proved advantage over morphine that is it is much more lipid soluble T"},{"label":"C","text":"Has plasma half life of 3 min T"},{"label":"D","text":"Is metabolized in the body to morphine and to 6-acetylmorphine T"},{"label":"E","text":"Is used for severe cough T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"1f38eaa535786dd36552fc76365663a662bc7a7d"},{"id":"q-f4c257d9902c","stem":"Lignocaine; a local anaesthetic","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Anaesthetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"prevents the rapid inflow of sodium ions which is the ionic basis of action potential T"},{"label":"B","text":"causes vasoconstriction F"},{"label":"C","text":"is not absorbed from the urethra F"},{"label":"D","text":"can be combined with adrenaline for digital ring blocks F"},{"label":"E","text":"has shorter duration of action than bupivacaine T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"4ab040431a1be72ebbba947432d833f0b136004d"},{"id":"q-7c4c29669ae3","stem":"The following transmitter mainly increase neuronal activity in the CNS","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"glutamate T"},{"label":"B","text":"glycine F"},{"label":"C","text":"acetylcholine F"},{"label":"D","text":"GABA F"},{"label":"E","text":"Aspartate T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"f75609c624ec9854b1436f1a98adc20bc7e190cb"},{"id":"q-5d2eb54e8d8f","stem":"The following statements about convulsants agents are correct","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"strychnine is an antagonist at GABA receptors F"},{"label":"B","text":"strychnine poisoning is treated with short acting neuromuscular blocking agent T"},{"label":"C","text":"bicuculline is a competitive antagonist of GABA at GABA receptors T"},{"label":"D","text":"picrotoxin causes functional opposition T"},{"label":"E","text":"strychnine is an antagonist at glycine at its receptor T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8e21314666a164f71f9ae54feffca0396d6786e1"},{"id":"q-a2b05c7e88dc","stem":"The following statements about anticonvulsant agents are correct","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"CNS Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"the dextro – and levo- isomers of amphetamine have equal potency in the periphery T"},{"label":"B","text":"levo-amphetamine is twice as potent as a central stimulant F"},{"label":"C","text":"the treatment of acute poisoning of amphetamine involves alkalinization of urine to promote secretion F"},{"label":"D","text":"current clinical use of amphetamine is restricted to rarely conditions of narcolepsy T"},{"label":"E","text":"tranylcypromine is an inhibitorof MAO but causes more stimulation of CNS than amphetamine F"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"9f351067f676314bd3cd224d65bd243141597728"},{"id":"q-e5ae4ce0aa7c","stem":"The following statements about hallucinogens are correct","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Mescaline and related hallucinogens are potent hallucinogens. They cause vivid hallucinations though the mechanism is unknown T"},{"label":"B","text":"LSD cause vivid hallucination. The mechanism is presumed to stem from its ability to interact with neurotransmitter dopamine F"},{"label":"C","text":"Pentazocine cause dysphoria and visual hallucination that are commonly persecutory T"},{"label":"D","text":"Nitrous oxide cause auditory distortion. Mechanism may involve interaction with endorphin and enkephaline T"},{"label":"E","text":"Cocaine causes euphoria, indifference to pain and tactile hallucinations. Mechanism is presumed to be related to inhibition of MAO F"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"f91ca8e870fae2ddc79848611974d011a1cb1de9"},{"id":"q-8cee74f93810","stem":"Opiod peptides are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"components of opioid receptors to which morphine bind F"},{"label":"B","text":"molecules such as pentapeptides methionine-enkephaline and leucine enkephaline T"},{"label":"C","text":"found both in the CNS and in the periphery T"},{"label":"D","text":"a fragment containing 17 amino acids (dynorphin) some of the most potent agonist at the opiod receptors T"},{"label":"E","text":"antagonists morphine F"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"d7638cae1ed47c8b38878ef9bccfdd045dd3d6d5"},{"id":"q-8b63f38478f2","stem":"Symptoms that indicate phenytoin overdoses are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hirsutism. T"},{"label":"B","text":"Dysarthiria. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Megaloblastic anaemia. F"},{"label":"D","text":"Ataxia. T"},{"label":"E","text":"Gum hyperplasia. F"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"10fe9d0a424b8213cccd8915e55c43dca8a41248"},{"id":"q-384c5764022d","stem":"The mechanism of action of valproate include","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"inhibition of GABA transaminase T"},{"label":"B","text":"increase of Na conduction F"},{"label":"C","text":"reduction in the action glutamic acid and decarboxylase enzymes F"},{"label":"D","text":"activation of potassium channels T"},{"label":"E","text":"inhibition of calcium channels T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"df83336bc9484c1d8633903f19337724d0b51633"},{"id":"q-622a11017d21","stem":"The following have been matched with appropriate anticonvulsant","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"CNS Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Blockage of voltage gated sodium channels – valproate T"},{"label":"B","text":"Blockage of glutaminergic transmission – Phenytoin F"},{"label":"C","text":"Blockage of T-calcium channel – zonisamide T"},{"label":"D","text":"Increased GABAergic inhibition of neurotransmission- carbamazepine F"},{"label":"E","text":"Inhibition of GABA uptake – Tiagabine T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"c19212e1cdd34f1f9a4333145c1343cd1113912c"},{"id":"q-97ee19715b81","stem":"Inhaled anaesthetics","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Anaesthetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cause membrane polarization by activating potassium channels. F"},{"label":"B","text":"Increases the duration of opening of nicotinic receptors. F"},{"label":"C","text":"Depress evoked but not spontaneous activity of neurones. F"},{"label":"D","text":"Appear to interact with component of cell membranes. T"},{"label":"E","text":"Render the patient unresponsive to painful stimulation. T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"2724ac3e8d0fd49fbe319e14aec049feba8f3aec"},{"id":"q-563d6e67b640","stem":"Rapid induction of anaesthesia is caused by agents that are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Highly soluble in blood F"},{"label":"B","text":"Highly soluble in fat F"},{"label":"C","text":"Poorly soluble in blood T"},{"label":"D","text":"Poorly soluble in fat T"},{"label":"E","text":"Poorly extracted by the liver T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"76c56607289a52cf0d1dac09d65b2cd9ec15ef93"},{"id":"q-4f4a79f1d35e","stem":"Nitrous oxide","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is highly soluble in blood. F"},{"label":"B","text":"Has low potency. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Has an analgesic property. T"},{"label":"D","text":"Does not cause post-anaesthetic hypoxia. F"},{"label":"E","text":"May cause bone marrow depression. T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["short_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e5de997590b2","stem":"In comparison with nitrous oxide, halothane","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is more potent T"},{"label":"B","text":"Produces a greater analgesic effect F"},{"label":"C","text":"Is more soluble in blood T"},{"label":"D","text":"Produces a faster induction of anaesthesia F"},{"label":"E","text":"Is more likely to reduce blood pressure T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"af2961a4f8ab51c56ca2f3a49ba57f86754f03b3"},{"id":"q-ac8b6df01c55","stem":"Sodium valproate","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is a dopamine agonist F"},{"label":"B","text":"Is indicated in tonic clonic epilepsy T"},{"label":"C","text":"The commonest adverse effect are dizziness and sedation F"},{"label":"D","text":"Is teratogenic T"},{"label":"E","text":"Is safe in pregnancy F"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"bf20b4dd053301b8b05ca2762c3209d6a40ef5f3"},{"id":"q-a80b694e353d","stem":"Hypnotic drugs","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is a new hypnotic agent without sedative or anxiolytic properties"},{"label":"B","text":"Decreases the time for sleep onset and also maintains sleep well. 32 ZOLDIPEM"},{"label":"C","text":"Has a receptor antagonist (flumazenil) to treat overdoses. 1(benzo)"},{"label":"D","text":"Induces P450 metabolism other drugs. 24 SECOBARBITOL"},{"label":"E","text":"Is a melatonin receptor agonist. 21 RAMELTON"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","short_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"1db8cff55439dac00c5a04cbf1e5e3c747cf747a"},{"id":"q-b75fda16cdb4","stem":"Drugs to treat bipolar disorder","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is used for bipolar disorder although it has high toxicity. 17 LITHIUM"},{"label":"B","text":"Is used to treat only the depressive phase of bipolar. 15 LAMOTRIGINE"},{"label":"C","text":"Has efficacy alone and is well tolerated. 29 VALPROATE"},{"label":"D","text":"Is used in combination with lithium, with fewer or equal side effects. 6 CABAMAZEPINE"},{"label":"E","text":"Should be monitored for plasma blood levels. 17 LITHIUM"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"decbed5734c463c5d6c4016ee5ba45bd021c2ea3"},{"id":"q-e346b1b43706","stem":"Drugs to treat movement disorders","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is a dopamine agonist effective in later stages of parkinsonism 3 BROMOCRIPTINE"},{"label":"B","text":"Is a pro drug that is most effective in early stages of parkinsonism 16 LEVODOPA"},{"label":"C","text":"Is an antimuscarinic agent used to treat Parkinson's disease 28 TRIHEXYPHENIDYL"},{"label":"D","text":"Is an antiviral drug used to treat Parkinson disease 2 AMANTADINE"},{"label":"E","text":"Is an MAO-B inhibitor used to treat Parkinson's disease 25 SELEGILINE"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"cb39ff29d3aa37f575239a633a8e09ccd33a7d7c"},{"id":"q-37184c23efdb","stem":"Antipsychotic (neuroleptic drugs)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Antipsychotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is an antipsychotic drug with few extra pyramidal/other side effects 23 RESERPERIDONE"},{"label":"B","text":"Has a longer duration of action than suggested by its half life 12 HALOPERIDAL"},{"label":"C","text":"Is available in one or more forms of acute psychosis 12 HALOPERIDAL"},{"label":"D","text":"Is a neuroleptic which cause agranulocytosis and seizures 9 CLOZAPINE"},{"label":"E","text":"May cause parkinsonism, neuroleptic malignant disorders or tardive dyskinesia 12 HALOPERIDAL"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a21334cbe00c60ce68f56521b570ffab5a50de99"},{"id":"q-374981b70c87","stem":"Anxiolytic drugs","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is effective for panic disorders 1 ALPRAZOLAM"},{"label":"B","text":"Is an alcohol used by institutions due to its low cost 8 CLORAL HYDRATE"},{"label":"C","text":"Does not have the abuse liability of barbiturates on benzodiazepine 5 BUSPIRONE"},{"label":"D","text":"Is used for situational anxiety 20 PROPRANOLOL"},{"label":"E","text":"Is an anxiolytic drug without hypnotic properties 25 SELEGILINE"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"8de3278bfe96fa8c2a3564db8885d8a1f37e122e"},{"id":"q-f49cbe91616e","stem":"Drugs used to treat movement disorders","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is effective for physiologic(postural) and essential (familiar) tremors 20 PROPRANOLOL"},{"label":"B","text":"Is a chelator used in Wilson's disease and rheumatoid arthritis 19 PENICILAMINE"},{"label":"C","text":"Is an antipsychotic drug that is also used for Huntington's disease 12 HALOPERIDAL"},{"label":"D","text":"Is used in Wilson's disease to decrease GI absorption of copper 31 ZINC SULPHATE"},{"label":"E","text":"Is used in huntington's chorea to deplete dopamine from vesicles 22 RESERPINE"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"public