,"answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f3345f359351","stem":"Which DNA polymerase inhibitor is used only for life –or sight threatening Cytomegalovirus infection in patient who is immuno-compromised? And why? (4marks)","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"e64f2e62c78f558fe112f78d2e7f63ac8307955b"},{"id":"q-08c59d1517be","stem":"Drugs used to treat hormone-dependent cancers","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Tamoxifen is a SERM for breast cancer"},{"label":"B","text":"Finasteride is an androgen receptor antagonist"},{"label":"C","text":"Flutamide is an α-reductase inhibitor"},{"label":"D","text":"Aminoglutethimide is an aromatase inhibitor"},{"label":"E","text":"Tamoxiphen can cause endometrial cancer"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b1719c294257","stem":"Drugs used to treat infertility","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bromocriptine is a dopamine antagonist"},{"label":"B","text":"Urofollitropin is FSH from postmenopausal urine"},{"label":"C","text":"hCG is used to treat male and female infertility"},{"label":"D","text":"clomifene is a SERM used to treat infertility"},{"label":"E","text":"Leuprolide is a GnRH agonist used to treat infertility"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"bf02c172c1fdb26c87a0c2aceb93b015afa55c51"},{"id":"q-fcae7814a7fa","stem":"Drugs with actions related to labor","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Oxytocin is a peptide hormone used to inhibit labor"},{"label":"B","text":"Indomethacin is an NSAID used to induce labor"},{"label":"C","text":"Misoprostol is prostaglandin E1 analog for inducing therapeutic abortion"},{"label":"D","text":"Carboprost is prostaglandin E2α used to control postpartum hemorrhage"},{"label":"E","text":"Mifepristone is a progesterone receptor antagonist pretreatment for therapeutic abortion"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d7be590bcda4","stem":"Thyroid drugs","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"131I concentrates in the thyroid, but cannot destroy this gland"},{"label":"B","text":"Propylthiouracil is the thioamine preferred for pregnant patients and inhibits 5’-deiodinase"},{"label":"C","text":"Carbimazole is a prodrug of methimazole"},{"label":"D","text":"Iodide has transient efficacy, but is useful pretreatment for thyroid surgery"},{"label":"E","text":"Thiocyanate is a radiopaque material with limited use due to the risk of aplastic anemia"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["short_or_incomplete_stem","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["short_or_incomplete_stem","short_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-10c12d038ce5","stem":"Principles of pharmacology include","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A noncompetitive antagonist binds irreversibly to its receptor"},{"label":"B","text":"The fractional occupancy of a receptor predicts the extent of the effect of a drug"},{"label":"C","text":"The efficacy of a drug is its EC50"},{"label":"D","text":"Receptors can be enzymes that affect cell metabolism, growth, or differentiation"},{"label":"E","text":"Receptors may be intracellular, such as steroid hormone receptors, which alter gene expression"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f6bcb62129e2","stem":"Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be antipyretic or analgesic"},{"label":"B","text":"Treat inflammation, including paracetamol"},{"label":"C","text":"Include aspirin, which is indicated for treatment of viral fever in children"},{"label":"D","text":"Include ibuprofen, which has toxicity less than or equal to aspirin"},{"label":"E","text":"Can cause GI irritation"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"e8ec9f2d681604a86f0d338bfbb42fb2b530b09d"},{"id":"q-952e6a981fd8","stem":"Drug treatments for RA include","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucocorticoids,, which have efficacy only due to their anti-inflammatory actions"},{"label":"B","text":"Glucocorticoids, which can be replaced suddenly with another drug if the GC is not effective"},{"label":"C","text":"Cytotoxics, which bind to DNA or are incorporated into DNA, blocking DNA replication"},{"label":"D","text":"Cytotoxics, which can cause alopecia (hair loss) or affect hematopoietic or GI cells"},{"label":"E","text":"Drugs that block IL-2 production; these drugs target T lymphocytes"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7d36306181a2","stem":"Acute gout is treated with","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Glucocorticoids as first choice"},{"label":"B","text":"Allopurinol"},{"label":"C","text":"Colchicine"},{"label":"D","text":"Sulfinpyrazone"},{"label":"E","text":"Indomethacin"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"2bd9b6fa6fce39f5cdbf6d2b35320393dee60b7f"},{"id":"q-262c48a0548b","stem":"Drugs used to treat movement disorders","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bromocriptine is effective in only the early stages of Parkinson’s disease"},{"label":"B","text":"Levodopa is a prodrug that is most effective in the later stages of Parkinson’s disease"},{"label":"C","text":"Propranolol is effective for physiological (postural) and essential (familial) tremors"},{"label":"D","text":"Penicillamine is a chelator used for Wilson’s disease and for rheumatoid arthritis"},{"label":"E","text":"Haloperidol is an antipsychotic drug that is also used for Huntington’s chorea and tics"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"553a4f0fb1f8cf148fccb8e265c37e1bc86aec2a"},{"id":"q-5a06b80da302","stem":"Antipsychotic (neuroleptic) drugs","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Antipsychotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Risperidone has few extrapyramidal or other side effects"},{"label":"B","text":"Haloperidol has anticholinergic and antiadrenergic side effects"},{"label":"C","text":"Haloperidol is available in an intramuscular form for acute psychosis"},{"label":"D","text":"Haloperidol can cause agranulocytosis or seizures"},{"label":"E","text":"Clozapinel can cause parkinsonism, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, or tardive dyskinesia"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"a21334cbe00c60ce68f56521b570ffab5a50de99"},{"id":"q-cc674c5874f1","stem":"Sedative-hypnotic drugs","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alprazolam is not effective for panic disorder"},{"label":"B","text":"Chloral hydrate is an alcohol used by institutions due to its low cost"},{"label":"C","text":"Buspirone does not have the abuse liability of barbiturates or BZs"},{"label":"D","text":"Buspirone is an anxiolytic drug without hypnotic properties"},{"label":"E","text":"Triazolam has a receptor antagonist (flumazenil) to treat overdoses"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b1ab55314a20","stem":"Toxicology principles","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hypersensitivity reactions require prior exposure to the toxin to elicit a response"},{"label":"B","text":"Pesticides and nerve gases cause toxicity by interacting with enzymes or receptors"},{"label":"C","text":"Organ-related toxicity includes hepatotoxicity, which can be caused by acetaminophen"},{"label":"D","text":"Carcinogenesis is less likely to occur in immunosuppressed patients"},{"label":"E","text":"Cell death can involve apoptosis, which involves cell swelling"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8470885374ca","stem":"Treatment of overdoses or poisoning may include","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemoperfusion for inorganic salts"},{"label":"B","text":"Fresh frozen plasma for coumadin"},{"label":"C","text":"Methylene blue for nitrites"},{"label":"D","text":"Penicillamine for lead poisoning"},{"label":"E","text":"Flumazenil for barbiturate overdose F"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"394edd274b657981b373269200121d067ab0bbe2"},{"id":"q-9c7059dffa77","stem":"Drug-drug interactions","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Will not occur in the gut if the drugs are administered at least two hours apart"},{"label":"B","text":"Can be beneficial"},{"label":"C","text":"Often involve induction or inhibition of the metabolism of a drug by another drug"},{"label":"D","text":"Are likely to be adverse in patients who are seriously ill"},{"label":"E","text":"Are likely to be clinically important in patients with impaired renal or hepatic function"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0542341a6d7d","stem":"The following drugs given during pregnancy may have adverse effects on the fetus","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Zidovudine"},{"label":"B","text":"Tetracyclines"},{"label":"C","text":"Thalidomide"},{"label":"D","text":"Alcohol"},{"label":"E","text":"Retinoids"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3d8992c94214","stem":"Pediatric pharmacology","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pharmacokinetic differences in children include decreased body water compared to adults"},{"label":"B","text":"Pharmacokinetic differences in children include increased fat compared to adults"},{"label":"C","text":"Pediatric drug dosages are most reliable when they are calculated based on surface area"},{"label":"D","text":"Breastfeeding is contraindicated if the mother is receiving cancer chemotherapy"},{"label":"E","text":"Newborns should receive higher doses of topical drugs due to poor dermal penetration"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9e99e0e83cf4","stem":"Geriatric pharmacology","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Changes in the elderly that alter drug dosing include decreased renal excretion"},{"label":"B","text":"Changes in the elderly that alter drug dosing include decreased pulmonary excretion"},{"label":"C","text":"The elderly have increased susceptibility to orthostatic hypotension"},{"label":"D","text":"The elderly experience decreased adverse effects of diuretics"},{"label":"E","text":"The elderly show decreased susceptibility to respiratory depression"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e1fc39efcbae","stem":"Effects of disease and obesity on drugs include","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Altered drug absorption in the presence of migraine"},{"label":"B","text":"Altered drug metabolism by diabetes"},{"label":"C","text":"Decreased cardiac output can alter drug elimination"},{"label":"D","text":"Obesity can increase the volume of distribution of drugs"},{"label":"E","text":"Ascites fluid or pleural effusions can decrease drug half-lives"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-62f7af1956a2","stem":"Protein and gene therapeutics","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Protein therapy involves giving proteins isolated from cell cultures or plants"},{"label":"B","text":"Protein therapy is shifting to recombinant proteins to increase the quantity of protein and decrease the risk of handling human tissue"},{"label":"C","text":"Gene therapy involves giving DNA or RNA to patients"},{"label":"D","text":"Gene therapeutics are never administered in a virus vector"},{"label":"E","text":"Gene therapeutics include the drug fomivirsen for retinitis from cytomegalovirus in AIDS patients"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f5f52db10074","stem":"Clinical trials","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Are regulated by ethics that are standardized internationally"},{"label":"B","text":"Are monitored by a local review board or ethics committee"},{"label":"C","text":"Will be stopped if patients are enrolled without giving informed consent"},{"label":"D","text":"Are conducted to assess the efficacy of a new drug, but not its toxicity"},{"label":"E","text":"Are unlikely to be conducted in Tanzania STUDENT VERSION MD III Students Clinical Pharmacology 2007-2008 FINAL EXAM"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"3c8b89c5631ef2b52584b3f60d3570314bc6784b"},{"id":"q-ccdfb68d325c","stem":"Intravenous verapamil","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"May terminate supraventricular tachycardia."},{"label":"B","text":"Must not be given to patients receiving β-blockers."},{"label":"C","text":"Reduces digoxin excretion."},{"label":"D","text":"One must delay DC cardio version at least 2 hours after a dose."},{"label":"E","text":"Shortens the PR interval."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"b2be618938eec53b9f8a5c63e9b90664c72f6ebc"},{"id":"q-94b9aa55ce0b","stem":"Nifedipine in comparison to verapamil is more likely to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Worsen angina."},{"label":"B","text":"Cause ankle swelling unresponsive to diuretics"},{"label":"C","text":"Have negative inotropic effects."},{"label":"D","text":"Cause flushing and headache."},{"label":"E","text":"Cause constipation."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"19c09b47128faec7c8c69f64a9ccb1848b3b632d"},{"id":"q-6d2082d99246","stem":"Insulin causes","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Endocrine Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Endocrine Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Reduction of hepatic output of glucose."},{"label":"B","text":"Reduction of protein synthesis."},{"label":"C","text":"Enhanced transit of potassium into cells."},{"label":"D","text":"Increased glucose uptake in peripheral tissues."},{"label":"E","text":"Down regulation of insulin receptors when its concentration is high; a factor in the insulin resistance of obese diabetics."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Endocrine Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","short_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"da78919f35f45898cbd29370b85a5476d035d5c2"},{"id":"q-39906c02f71d","stem":"The following statements about therapy with iron are true","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Parenteral administration produces a more rapid rise in haemoglobin than oral administration."},{"label":"B","text":"Desferrioxamine is useful in the treatment of overdosage with iron preparations."},{"label":"C","text":"Ascorbic acid impairs the efficiency of iron absorption."},{"label":"D","text":"Iron preparation should not be taken at same time as with tea which contains tannate."},{"label":"E","text":"Gastro-intestinal side-effects often limit the use of oral iron preparations."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b6a6d5292e6a","stem":"Folic acid","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Alone provides adequate treatment for some cases of pernicious anaemia."},{"label":"B","text":"Is absorbed in the large intestine."},{"label":"C","text":"Is only active when converted to tetrahydrofolic acid."},{"label":"D","text":"Is not present in green vegetables other than spinach."},{"label":"E","text":"Should be used routinely in pregnancy in a dose of about 300 μg a day."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["short_or_incomplete_stem","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["short_or_incomplete_stem","short_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-70116138ce76","stem":"In the clinical management of migraine","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pizotifen is used prophylactically."},{"label":"B","text":"Ergotamine powder may be administered by aerosol inhaler."},{"label":"C","text":"Aspirin is value in mild attacks."},{"label":"D","text":"Headache may be a side-effect of ergotamine."},{"label":"E","text":"The hormonal contraceptive pill increases the frequency of attacks in some women."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6ab7925abcaa","stem":"Recombinant human erythropoietin is used to treat","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iron deficient anemia when iron malabsorbed."},{"label":"B","text":"Pernicious anemia."},{"label":"C","text":"Anaemia of chronic failure."},{"label":"D","text":"Zidovudine- induced anemia."},{"label":"E","text":"Clozapine-induced agranulocytosis."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"31b2146342ad903ae011d655decf9c99fa17452d"},{"id":"q-916e35909f22","stem":"Filgrastim (human granulocyte colony stimulating factor)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is usually administered by subcutaneous injection."},{"label":"B","text":"Causes immediate transient neutropenia."},{"label":"C","text":"Is used in myeloid leukemia."},{"label":"D","text":"Stimulated proliferation and differentiation of progenitor cells of all granulocyte lines."},{"label":"E","text":"Causes bone pain. OPTIONS FOR QUESTION 16 T0 21 |1Aminoglutethimide |8.Ethinyloestradiol |15. indomethacin |22.misoprostol | |2. Bromocriptine |9. Ergometrine |16. iodide |23. norethindrone | |3. Carbimazole |10. Finasteride |17. iopanoic acid |24. oxytocin | |4. Carboprost |11. flutamide |18. leuprolide |25.propylthiouracil | |5. Clomifene |12. gonadorelin |19. levothyroxine |26. tamoxifen | |6.Cyproterone |13. hCG |20. Methimazole | 27. testosterone | |7. Dinoprost |14. 131I |21. mifepristone |28. thiocyanate | | | | |29. urofollitropin | Write the number of the drug that is described below. Drugs may be used more than once. Some drugs above may not be used. 16Drugs used to treat hormone-dependent cancers: _____(a) this drug is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) _____(b) is an androgen receptor antagonist _____(c) is an aromatase inhibitor _____(d) is a GnRH agonist _____(e) is an α-reductase inhibitor 17Drugs used to treat infertility: _____(a) is used to treat male and female infertility _____(b) is a SERM _____(c) is a dopamine agonist _____(d) is FSH from postmenopausal urine _____(e) is an LH (luteinizing hormone) agonist 18These drugs have the following properties: _____(a) is used to treat female hirsutism _____(b) is not orally active due to inactivation by first pass metabolism _____(c) is in a formulation that provides a pulsatile pattern of drug delivery _____(d) is available as a yearly implant _____(e) is not orally active because it is a peptide 19Drugs used during labor: _____(a) is a prostaglandin (PGE1) analogue for inducing therapeutic abortion _____(b) is a prostaglandin (PGF2α) used to control postpartum hemorrhage _____(c) is a peptide hormone used to induce labor _____(d) is a progesterone receptor antagonist pretreatment for therapeutic abortion _____(e) is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) used to inhibit labor 20These drugs produce the following side effects: _____(a) endometrial cancer _____(b) ovarian hyperstimulation syndrome _____(c) gall bladder disease _____(d) acne, weight gain, hirsutism (virilization) _____(e) asthma 21Antithyroid drugs: _____(a) is the thioamine preferred for pregnant patients and inhibits 5’-deiodinase _____(b) concentrates in the thyroid, destroying this gland _____(c) has transient efficacy, but is useful pretreatment for thyroid surgery _____(d) this radiopaque material has limited use due to the risk of aplastic anemia _____(e) is a prodrug of methimazole In questions 22 to 60 indicate whether the option following the initial statement is true (T) or false (F). (22) Corticosteroids when used in ulcerative colitis-: (a) Inhibit immune response of B-cells and macrophages. (b) Require dose reduction as symptoms improve. (c) Are used in long-term as it is in Crohn’s conditions. (d) May precipitate hyperglycaemia in diabetic patients. (e) May make an individual susceptible to infections. (23) Domperidone-: (a) Is a D2 antagonist. (b) Is contraindicated in Parkinson’s disease. (c) Causes amenorrhea. (d) Produces cardiac dysrhythmias. (e) More potent than metoclopramide. (24) Codeine phosphate-: (a) Has antiemetic, antittusive, analgesic and antidiarrhoea effects. (b) Acts on μ and k-receptors. (c) In diarrhoea it causes decrease GIT motility and reduced secretion. (d) Is not effective in severe diarrhoea. (e) Is an opioid derivative. (25) Loperamide-: (a) Is a natural opiate (opioid derivative). (b) Penetrates well to the CNS. (c) Used in management of traveler’s diarrhoea when combined with antimicrobials. (d) Has t1⁄2 = 8 hours. (e) is equally potent as morphine in management of diarrhoea. (26) Cimetidine-: (a) Is a potent H2-receptor antagonist. (b) Is associated with gynaecomastia and increased libido. (c) May cause mental confusion in elderly patients. (d) Is a potent enzyme inducer. (e) Is less effective in reducing food stimulated acid secretion. (27) Omeprazole-: (a) Is a proton pump inhibitor. (b) Is normally formulated as delayed release capsules. (c) Is given orally where by the normal dose is 60 mg daily. (d) Protein binding capacity is 75%. (e) Is a benzamide derivative. (28) Pirenzepine-: (a) Antagonizes muscarinic receptors (M2-type). (b) Lacks effects on gastric acid secretion. (c) is a new drug. (d) May also have a cytoprotective effect on the GIT mucosa. (e) Is associated with salivary secretions increase and constipation. (29) Misoprostol-: (a) Is a prostaglandin I2 analogue. (b) Is a second line agent in management of peptic ulcers. (c) Is used to prevent gastric ulcers disease in patients who must take aspirin. (d) Is contraindicated in pregnancy. (e)All of the above are correct. (30) Sumatriptan-: (a) Is a 5HT3 agonist. (b) Is not as potent as Ergometrine in reducing migraine symptoms. (c) Is associated with many side effects. (d) Is contraindicated in ischaemic heart disease. (e) Is a potent vasodilator. (31) Heparin-: (a) Cannot pass through water filled pores within the gastro-intestinal mucosa. (b) Antagonizes the action of antithrombin 111. (c) Causes osteomalacia when used in long term therapy. (d) Is excreted in the urine entirely unchanged. (e) Is only admitted parenterally for systemic effects."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review","repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"d083e1205591f51de54743020de1d21319232e06"},{"id":"q-13c22e62ce19","stem":"Tranexamic acid","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Inhibits the action of plasmin."},{"label":"B","text":"Is orally active."},{"label":"C","text":"Should not be given to patients who are at risk of bleeding."},{"label":"D","text":"Is useful in treatment of upper gastro-intestinal haemorrhage."},{"label":"E","text":"Inhibits the action of pepsin."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"87e1730ef1aa3678005