s in","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Increased susceptibility to opportunistic infections"},{"label":"B","text":"Hyperkalemia"},{"label":"C","text":"Hypertemsion"},{"label":"D","text":"Posterior capsula cataracts"},{"label":"E","text":"Proximal myopathy"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dfe75e61fde1","stem":"Adverse effects associated with Cyclosporin include","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nephrotoxicity"},{"label":"B","text":"Nausea and GI disturbance"},{"label":"C","text":"AlopesiaF"},{"label":"D","text":"Tremor"},{"label":"E","text":"HypokalemiaF"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b299b106b7dd","stem":"Which of the following statements are correct?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A useful drug must act selectively on particular cells or tissues (T)"},{"label":"B","text":"Proteins that act as drug target generally show a low degree of ligand specificity (F)"},{"label":"C","text":"A small chemical change in a molecule e.g. alteration of one amino acid can inactivate the molecule (T)"},{"label":"D","text":"A great majority of drugs act with complete specificity (F)"},{"label":"E","text":"Ion channels are enzymes commonly involved as primary targets for drug action F"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-19eed0daa1de","stem":"Which of the following statements are true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A receptor is defined as a transmembrane protein that can recognise and bind chemical messengers that then trigger the cell’s response T"},{"label":"B","text":"Some receptors are not in the plasma membrane but in the cytoplasm or nucleus (T)"},{"label":"C","text":"An agonist may be defined as a ligand that binds to receptors, altering a proportion of them that are active and thus triggering the cell’s response T"},{"label":"D","text":"An antagonist is defined as a ligand that binds to a receptor, thus interfering with agonist action by preventing it from binding T"},{"label":"E","text":"A reversible competitive antagonist causes parallel displacement of the the gonist concentration-response curve to the left without without reduction of the maximum (F)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"6bf9ead4ae2bf3e9e3630756a8163f216bc34034"},{"id":"q-6349130b00ec","stem":"Which of the following statements about receptors are true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Enzymes can be considered as receptors (F)"},{"label":"B","text":"Receptors may be linked to DNA by G proteins (F)"},{"label":"C","text":"A receptor may have enzymatic activity (T)"},{"label":"D","text":"Receptors may be linked to ion channels by G proteins (T)"},{"label":"E","text":"Receptors may have an intergral ion channel (T)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8a684f60c054","stem":"Which categories of chemicals interact with receptors – either as agonists or antagonists?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Neurotransmitters (T)"},{"label":"B","text":"Hormones (T)"},{"label":"C","text":"Enzymes (F)"},{"label":"D","text":"Drugs (T)"},{"label":"E","text":"Inflammatory mediators (T)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0774aae3dbd9","stem":"Which mechanisms are important in controlling intracellular concentration of Ca2+","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Efflux of Ca2+ from the cell through voltage-gated calcium channels (F)"},{"label":"B","text":"Release from endoplasmic reticulum (T)"},{"label":"C","text":"Active transport of Ca2+ into the endoplasmic reticulum (T)"},{"label":"D","text":"Release of Ca2+ from mitochondria via inostol triphosphate (IP3) receptors (F)"},{"label":"E","text":"Transport across the plasma membrave via a Na+/ Ca2+ (T)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-94b4f903e9cd","stem":"Which statements are true of drug effects on excitable cells?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"an increase in neuronal plasma membrane permeability to Cl- is responsible for the antiepileptic activity of benzodiazepines (T)"},{"label":"B","text":"local anaesthetics inhibit action potential generation by blocking voltage-gated Na+ channels (T)"},{"label":"C","text":"the sulphonylurea hypoglycaemic agents block activation of K+ channels in B-cells of the islets of Langerhans (T)"},{"label":"D","text":"Nifedipine ( a dihydropyridine) inhibits smooth musle relaxiation by blocking Na+/ Ca2+ exchange (F)"},{"label":"E","text":"Suxamethonium depolarises the endplate region of skeletal muscle by activating nicotinic receptors (T)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e5fd77f46feb","stem":"Which of the following statements about nitric acid are true?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitric oxide is synthesised from the amino acid lysine (F)(ARGENINE)"},{"label":"B","text":"Nitric oxide activates guanylate cyclase in vascualr smooth muscle (T)"},{"label":"C","text":"Nitric oxide mediates the vascular smooth muscle relaxiation produced by glyceryl trinitrate (T)"},{"label":"D","text":"Nitric oxide has cytotoxic effect on pathogens (T)"},{"label":"E","text":"Nitric oxide can be converted to peroxynitrite (T)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7f53f1dc3b62","stem":"Which statements are true of local anaesthetics?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Anaesthetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"they prevent or relieve pain by interrupting action potential propagation (T)"},{"label":"B","text":"they decrease the threshold for electrical excitability as block develops (F)"},{"label":"C","text":"they block conduction irreversible by binding to sodium channels F"},{"label":"D","text":"they can produce both sensory and motor paralysis in the area innervated (F)"},{"label":"E","text":"they act on every type of nerve fibre (F)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-45c6be757b1d","stem":"which ONE of the local anaesthetics listed below has the shortest duration of action?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Anaesthetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Bupivacaine (F)"},{"label":"B","text":"lidocaine (F)"},{"label":"C","text":"amethocaine (F)"},{"label":"D","text":"prilocaine (F)"},{"label":"E","text":"procaine (T)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a9fbe4320f66","stem":"which statements are true of histamine and antihistamine drugs?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"histamine has a role in the increased gastric acid secretion associated with peptic ulcer (T)"},{"label":"B","text":"mepyramine is an H2-receptor antagonist (F)"},{"label":"C","text":"cimetidine is an H1-receptor agonist (F)"},{"label":"D","text":"H2-receptor antagonists are used mainly to treat allergic reactions (F)"},{"label":"E","text":"H1-receptor antagonists are first-line drugs for the treatment of the first phase of the asthmatic attack (F)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4116e49de525","stem":"which are pharmacologically active mediators that are derived locally from cells, and contribute to the development of the local acute inflammatory reaction?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Histamine (T)"},{"label":"B","text":"bradykinin (F)(CIRCULATING)"},{"label":"C","text":"cholecystokinin F"},{"label":"D","text":"Calcitonin-gene-related peptide (CGRP) (T)"},{"label":"E","text":"Metenkephalin (F)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-253f6bb8152c","stem":"Which facts are true of cyclo-oxygenase?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"it acts directly on arachidonate to produce the prostanoids and thromboxanes (F)"},{"label":"B","text":"there are two isoforms: cyclo-oxygenase-1 and cyclo-oxygenase-2 (T)"},{"label":"C","text":"Cyclo-oxygenase-1 is a constitutive enzyme found in most cells and important in normal homeostasis (T)"},{"label":"D","text":"Cyclo-oxygenase-2 is induced in inflammatory cells by inflammatory stimuli (T)"},{"label":"E","text":"Mmnlnl"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9fd6882b14d2","stem":"which statements are true of eicosanoids?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"thromboxane A2 is a vasodilator (F)"},{"label":"B","text":"prostaglandin E2 stimulates gastric secretion (F)"},{"label":"C","text":"prostaglandin I2 directly increases the permeability of post-capillary venules (F)(LEUCOTRIENE)"},{"label":"D","text":"prostaglandin I2 is a vasodilator and inhibits platelet aggregation (T)"},{"label":"E","text":"Prostaglandin F2( relaxes uterine muscle (F)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ede6a4fabf3c","stem":"which are true of the glucocorticoids used clinically?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Endocrine Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Endocrine Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"predinisolone if the first-line drug for anti-inflammatory action (T)"},{"label":"B","text":"cortisone is particular after topical application (F)"},{"label":"C","text":"hydrocortisone is a first-line drug for treatment of adrenocortical insufficiency (T)(HYDROCORTISONE=COTISOL)"},{"label":"D","text":"fluticasone is given orally or intravenously for asthma (F)"},{"label":"E","text":"dexamethasone is used in the treatment of tumour-induced cerebral oedema (T)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Endocrine Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8974f3554a8c","stem":"which statements are true of the corticosteroid hormones secreted from the adrenal cortex","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"the corticosteroids are stored, preformed, in the adrenal cortex (F)"},{"label":"B","text":"Corticotrophin (ACTH) stimulates both synthesis and release of glucocorticoids (T)"},{"label":"C","text":"All corticosteroids have a negative feedback effect on corticotrophin (ACTH) release T"},{"label":"D","text":"Agiotensin II acts on the adrenal cortex to release the endogenous mineralocorticoid, aldosterone (T)"},{"label":"E","text":"A deficiency of corticosteroids is the basis of Cushing’s disease (F)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a1f3383bae8f","stem":"which class of compounds are used to treat anxiety?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors F"},{"label":"B","text":"Benzodiazepine-receptor agonists (T)"},{"label":"C","text":"5-HT1A-receptor agonists (T)(BUSPIRONE)"},{"label":"D","text":"(-adrenoceptor antagonists (T)"},{"label":"E","text":"monoamine oxidase inhibitors (F)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1c924e4077ad","stem":"which are clinical uses of the benzodiazepines?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"CNS Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"treatment of depression (F)"},{"label":"B","text":"as anticonvulsants (T)"},{"label":"C","text":"for preoperative sedation (T)"},{"label":"D","text":"treatment of anxiety disorders T"},{"label":"E","text":"as muscle relaxants in patients with chronic muscle spasms T(STATUS EPILEPTICUS)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"CNS Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ed180feebc7d","stem":"which statements are true of the barbiturates?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"they act by inhibiting the action of GABA on GABAA receptors (F)"},{"label":"B","text":"they cause nonspecific depression throughout the cemtral nervous system (CNS) (T)"},{"label":"C","text":"they are potent inducers of the cytochrome P450-metabolising enzymes (T)"},{"label":"D","text":"thiopental has useful anxiolytic properties (F)(i.v anaesthetic)"},{"label":"E","text":"phenobarbitone is used extensively as a hypnotic because it is effective and safe (F)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f9ae46600094","stem":"which are the main categories of clinically useful antidepressant drugs?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Antidepressant","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) (T)"},{"label":"B","text":"Dopamine D2-receptor antagonists (F)"},{"label":"C","text":"Tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs) (T)"},{"label":"D","text":"Selective inhibitors of 5-HT uptake (SSRIs) (T)"},{"label":"E","text":"Lithium (T)(tx of bipolar disorder)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-952893d17f42","stem":"for a drug be useful as a general anaesthetic, which criteria (ideally) should be fulfilled?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Anaesthetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"induction should be smooth and rapid (T)"},{"label":"B","text":"recovery should be rapid and should comence as soon as administration has ceased (T)"},{"label":"C","text":"the anaesthesia should be controllable - allowing the level to be adjusted as necessary during the operative procedure (T)"},{"label":"D","text":"the anaesthetic should have a wide margin of safety (T)"},{"label":"E","text":"there should be no adverse effects (F)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-10d3067e042a","stem":"which statements are true of the effects of general anaesthetics on the respiratory or cardiovascular system?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","topicOriginal":"Central Nervous System Drugs","subtopic":"Anaesthetic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Nitorus oxide causes relaxation of vascular smooth muscle (F)(nitric oxide)"},{"label":"B","text":"Many halogenated anaesthetics cause cardiac dysrhythmias (T)"},{"label":"C","text":"Noradrenaline protects against dysrhythmias if given in the presence of halothane (F)"},{"label":"D","text":"Nearly all anaesthetics depress respiration markedly, and increase PCO2 (T)"},{"label":"E","text":"All anaesthetics decrease cardiac contractility (T)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Central Nervous System Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-53e6da840ad6","stem":"which statements are true of the anti-cancer drug cyclophosphamide?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"it is an alkylating agent and has a direct cytotoxic action on DNA T"},{"label":"B","text":"it forms covalent bonds with carbon atoms in the DNA (F)(nitrogen)"},{"label":"C","text":"because it has two alkylating groups it can cross-link the two DNA strands T"},{"label":"D","text":"it causes significant nausea and vomiting (T)(side effect main)"},{"label":"E","text":"it can cause haemorrhagic cystitis (T)(also ifophosphamide)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9abba2e2f10b","stem":"which of the following statements are true of chloramphenicol?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"is a semi-synthetic antibiotic (F)(non synthetic)"},{"label":"B","text":"inhibits bacterial DNA synthesis through inhibition of transpeptidation (F)(inhibit protein synth)"},{"label":"C","text":"is bactericidal for most organisms (F)"},{"label":"D","text":"the level of resistance to the drug is higher in Gram-negative bacteria than in Gram-positive bacteria (T)"},{"label":"E","text":"the most important unwanted effect is a dose-dependent severe idiosyncratic depression of the bone marrow (F)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-43172899e7b4","stem":"which statements are true of antifungal drugs?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antifungal","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Polyenes are naturally occuring antifungal antibiotics (T)"},{"label":"B","text":"Caspofungin is a member of the polyenes group (F)(ECHINOCANDIN)"},{"label":"C","text":"Rifampicin increases the absorption of ketoconazole (F)(also ant-acids n cimetidine impair absorption)"},{"label":"D","text":"Unlike ketoconazole, fluconazole, in doses usually used, can inhibit hepatic drug metabolism and steroidogenesis (F)"},{"label":"E","text":"Terbinafine is particularly useful against nail infections (T)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-44f5001106d9","stem":"which of the following statements about antiviral drugs are correct?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antiviral","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Abacavir is the least effective of the nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs) (F)"},{"label":"B","text":"Niverapine or zidovudine can prevent mother-to-child transmission of HIV if given to the parturient mother and to the newborn (T)"},{"label":"C","text":"Ganciclovir has serious unwanted effects and is used only for life-threatening CMV infections in immunocompromised patients (T)"},{"label":"D","text":"Enfurvirtide acts on a relatively new drug traget on CD4 or other Tcells and is now often included in the recommended first-line antiretroviral combination therapy (F)"},{"label":"E","text":"Palivisumab is a polycolonal antibody directed against a glycoprotein on the surface of RSV (F)(MONOCLONAL)"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"404f8cfbb5dc3ee37ab35ceedc2ec0934dc527c4"},{"id":"q-438b5eee4e7b","stem":"Do I prescribe antihypertensive drugs in all hypertensive patients?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c8e4e2b3bc68","stem":"How do I decide which antihypertensive drug to start with?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1254c7ae7327","stem":"Which potential side-effects of antipertensive drugs do I need to inform the patient about?","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-aa655ba69392","stem":"Which target blood pressure do I want to achieve with an antihypertensive therapy?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f4a0c54046cf","stem":"What do I do if the target blood pressure has not been reached by the primary antihypertensive therapy?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-368dd6a5277f","stem":"Which antihypertensive drugs can I combine and which combinations should I avoid?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-cf7e5e897578","stem":"How do I treat hypertension in the elderly?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c861827dd5ab","stem":"How do I treat a pregnancy-associated hypertension?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3b168617f1f8","stem":"How do I treat a hypertensive urgency/emergency?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-abfaa63120da","stem":"Which of the following is/are not indications for tetracyclines","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a664699f70a5","stem":"The clearance of a drug","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is the volume of plasma from which the drug is totally eliminated per unit time T"},{"label":"B","text":"Is equal to the administration rate at steady state divided by the steady state plasma concentration F"},{"label":"C","text":"Is a better measure of the effciency of drug elimination than elimination half-life T"},{"label":"D","text":"Does not include elimination by hepatic metabolism F"},{"label":"E","text":"May be affected by renal function T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-026370c78db6","stem":"For a drug that obeys first order (linear) kinetics and fits a one – compartment model of elimination","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Its rate of elimination is proportional to its plasma concentration T"},{"label":"B","text":"Following cessation of an intravenous infusion the plasm concentration declines exponentially T"},{"label":"C","text":"The half-life is proportional to the dose F"},{"label":"D","text":"The half-life is unaffected by renal function T (e)The composition of drug products excreted is independent of the dose T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dfc2de4e31dc","stem":"The apparent volume of distribution","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Can be greater than the total body volume T"},{"label":"B","text":"Is approximately 3 litres for most drugs in adults F"},{"label":"C","text":"Is influenced by a drug’s lipid solubility T"},{"label":"D","text":"A large value indicates that a drug will be efficiently eliminated by hemodialysis F"},{"label":"E","text":"Determines the peak plasma concentration after a bolus intravenous dose T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-39df56214d8c","stem":"The oral biovailability of a drug","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is a measure of the extent to which it enters the systemic circulation T"},{"label":"B","text":"May be influenced by changing the excipient from calcium sulphate to lactose T"},{"label":"C","text":"Is determined by comparing the area under the plasma concentration time curve (AUC) following oral and intravenous administration T"},{"label":"D","text":"May be reduced by hepatic enzyme induction T"},{"label":"E","text":"Two preparations of a drug may have similar bioavailability but have different peak concentrations T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ce8c2d032792","stem":"Drug absorption following oral administration","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is most commonly through passive diffusion T"},{"label":"B","text":"Occurs predominantly in the colon F"},{"label":"C","text":"Is usually complete within 60 minutes F(75%)"},{"label":"D","text":"Non-polar lipid-soluble drugs are absorbed more readily than polar water soluble drugs T"},{"label":"E","text":"Peptides are well absorbed following oral administration F"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-ccb1dfc77235","stem":"Intramuscular injection","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rate of absorption is enhanced by exercise T"},{"label":"B","text":"Rate of absorption is greater from the deltoid injection site than the gluteus maximus site F"},{"label":"C","text":"If administered to the buttock should be in the upper outer quadrant T"},{"label":"D","text":"Should usually be no greater than 0.5 ml F"},{"label":"E","text":"Is an appropriate route of administration for the decanoate ester of fluphenazine T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e8afd1fb9f9e","stem":"Monitoring drug concentrations of the following drugs is recognized as a valuable supplement to clinical monitoring","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cyclophosphamide T"},{"label":"B","text":"Warfarin T"},{"label":"C","text":"Gentamicin T"},{"label":"D","text":"Lithium T"},{"label":"E","text":"Cyclosporin F"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b69871eecb41","stem":"The following properties of a drug encourage their presence in breast milk","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"High lipid solubility T"},{"label":"B","text":"Unionized state T"},{"label":"C","text":"Low molecular weight T"},{"label":"D","text":"Weak base T"},{"label":"E","text":"Short half-life F"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-66a03eae4845","stem":"Type A adverse drug reactions","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Are not related to the main pharmacological action of the drug causing them F"},{"label":"B","text":"Are often reversible and respond to dose reduction T"},{"label":"C","text":"Are usually reasonably predictable T"},{"label":"D","text":"Are also termed as idiosyncratic reactions F"},{"label":"E","text":"Aplastic anaemia resulting from administration of chloramphenicol is an example of these drug reactions F"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e30a7ce63bae","stem":"Adverse drug effects unrelated to the main pharmacological action of the drug","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Have no plausible temporal relationship with the administration of the drug T"},{"label":"B","text":"Are often initiated by the parent drug rather than the metabolite F"},{"label":"C","text":"Are usually severe T"},{"label":"D","text":"May be predictable when the drug is taken during pregnancy T"},{"label":"E","text":"Are important in establishing the safety of medicines T 25 Regarding drug-induced nephrotoxicity"},{"label":"A","text":"Renal toxicity produced by ciclosporin is associated with systemic hypertension T"},{"label":"B","text":"NSAIDs can cause renal damage through multiple mechanisms of renal injury T"},{"label":"C","text":"Fenoprofen is less likely to cause interstitial nephritis than other NSAIDs T"},{"label":"D","text":"ACE inhibitors are potentially hazardous in patients with liver disease F"},{"label":"E","text":"Inhibition of renal prostaglandin biosynthesis is the established mechanism behind the pathogenesis of analgesic nephropathy T 26 In allergic reactions to drugs:"},{"label":"A","text":"Parent drugs but not their metabolites can bind covalently to proteins to form immunogens F"},{"label":"B","text":"Type I, II and IV drug-induced allergic (hypersensitivity) reactions are antibody mediated F(1 2 3"},{"label":"C","text":"Drug-induced allergic (hypersensitivity) reaction may result from doses that are too small to elicit pharmacodynamic effects T"},{"label":"D","text":"Anaphylactic shock associated with penicillin administration is the most important clinical type of allergic response to drugs T"},{"label":"E","text":"Heparin can cause anaphylaxis T 27 Regarding general management of drug over dosage or acute poisoning:"},{"label":"A","text":"Most deaths from poisoning occur in hospital F"},{"label":"B","text":"The overall hospital mortality from poisoning is very high F"},{"label":"C","text":"Resuscitation and management of poisoned patients differs very much from that of any other critically ill patient F"},{"label":"D","text":"The basic approach to management is to stop further absorption from the GI and enhancement of the elimination of the drug or poison T"},{"label":"E","text":"In many situations, treatment is influenced by the identification of the drug/poison and determining its plasma concentration T"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-99f993985fa6","stem":"Regarding drug absorption","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics","options":[{"label":"A","text":"When administered orally in solution, gastric emptying rate-limits the absorption of small lipophilic drugs.T"},{"label":"B","text":"For rapidly dissolving products of a drug, differences in rates of dissolution markedly affect the plasma concentration-time profile, when intestinal permeability is the rate-limit step. T"},{"label":"C","text":"Polar drugs are primarily absorbed from the small intestine via the transcellular route.F"},{"label":"D","text":"A substantial fraction of bioavailable drug enters the systemic circulation via the lymphatic route for large (M.W. greater than 100,000 g/mole) protein drugs administered intramuscularly.F"},{"label":"E","text":"Large nondisintegrating controlled-release dosage forms commonly remain in the stomach for 6 hr when taken just after a heavy meal.F 29 Salbutamol (a) Is a β2-adrenoceptor agonist T (b) Its primary effect in asthma is to inhibit the phosphodiesterase isoenzyme T (c) Is a physiological anatgonist of bronchoconstrictors T (d) Increases mucus clearance by an action on cilia T (e) Is used on an “as needed” basis to control symptoms of asthma T 30 Propranolol: (a) Is a Class II antidsyrhythmic drug T(Beta antagonists) (b) Should be used with caution in patients with diabetes mellitus or hyperthyroidism (c) Is one of the banned substances in the Olympics F (d) Co-administration with food tends to enhance bioavailability (e) Undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism 31 Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs: (a) Aspirin is currently mainly used for cardiovascular disease T (b) Celecoxib has fewer gastrointestinal effects T (c) The usual indications for Diclofenac include Rheumatic disease, musculoskeletal disorders and post operative pain T (d) Ibuprofen is suitable for children T (e) The usual indications of Piroxicam include Rheumatic disease, Gout and musculoskeletal disorders T 32 Enflurane: (a) Is an intravenous anaesthetic F(INHALED|) (b) Has higher risk of hepatoxicity than halothane F (c) May cause coronary ischemia in susceptible patients F(ISOFLURANE) (d) Has few adverse effects T(Halothane more toxic) (e) Is widely used as alternative for halothane T 33 Propofol:(MJ) (a) Is an intravenous anaesthetic T (b) Has fast onset but very slow recovery F (c) Is metabolized slowly F (d) Cardiovascular and respiratory depression is the main unwanted effect T (e) Is an inhalation anaesthetic F 34 The following are TRUE about anxiolytic and hypnotic drugs (a) Many anxiolytic drugs are also hypnotic T (b) Tricyclic antidepressants are suitable in depressed patients with sleep disturbance T(two birds one stone) (c) Most hypnotics act on specific modulatory sites on GABAA receptors and do not cause dependence T(use less than 4 wks) (d) Diazepam is a benzodiazepine, which is used as an anxiolytic and hypnotic agent with a long duration of action (24 – 48 h) F(NOT HYPNOTIC) (e) Benzodiazepines may paradoxically produce an increase in irritability and aggression in some individuals T 35 The following are FALSE about antipsychotic drugs: (a) The main side effects of chlorpromazine include obstructive jaundice F (b) All the extrapyramidal side effects associated with antipyschotic drugs result from D2-receptor blockade F (c) Haloperidol can rapidly control hyperactive psychotic states F (d) Clozapine is a second generation antipsychotic drug F (e) Flupenthixol can cause agranulocytosis T(CLOZAPINE) 36 Fluoxetine:"},{"label":"A","text":"Is a tricyclic antidepressant F"},{"label":"B","text":"Can be used together with imipramine for moderate to severe depression"},{"label":"C","text":"It is recommended for treating depression in children under 18 F"},{"label":"D","text":"Can be used to treat other psychiatric diseorders including anxiety disorders and panic attacks T"},{"label":"E","text":"In combination with Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors can cause tremor, hyperthermia and cardiovascular collapseT 37 Lamotrigine:"},{"label":"A","text":"Is a new antiepileptic drug T"},{"label":"B","text":"It has similar mechanism of action to that of phenytoin T"},{"label":"C","text":"Acts on potassium channels and inhibits release of axcitatory aminoacids F"},{"label":"D","text":"Has a broader therapeutic profile than phenytoin and carbamazepine T"},{"label":"E","text":"Can be used for the treatment of absence seizures T 38 Phenytoin:"},{"label":"A","text":"Is a major antiepileptic drug T"},{"label":"B","text":"Acts maily by use-dependent block of sodium channels T"},{"label":"C","text":"Is effective in many forms of epilepsy, but not in absence seizures T"},{"label":"D","text":"Its plasma concentration can vary widely between individuals T"},{"label":"E","text":"Is commonly associated with drug interactions T 39 Carbamazepine:"},{"label":"A","text":"Is a derivative of tricyclic antidepressants T"},{"label":"B","text":"Has fewer unwanted effects compared to phenytoin T"},{"label":"C","text":"It is effective in most forms of epilepsy including absence seizures F"},{"label":"D","text":"Plasma half-life shortens when it is given repeatedly T"},{"label":"E","text":"It is particularly effective in treating complex partial seizures T 40 Paracetamol:"},{"label":"A","text":"Unlike other NSAIDs lacks anti-inflammatory effects T"},{"label":"B","text":"Like other NSAIDs can cause gastric ulceration and bleeding F"},{"label":"C","text":"It is only a weak cyclo-oxygenase inhibitor with some selectivity to brain cyclo-oxygenase (COX) T"},{"label":"D","text":"Its metabolism involves both Phase I and Phase II metabolism T"},{"label":"E","text":"In overdose causes severe liver damage, which is commonly fatal T 41 Tramadol:"},{"label":"A","text":"Is used for the treatment of acute and chronic pain T"},{"label":"B","text":"Its route of administration is limited to oral F"},{"label":"C","text":"Respiratory depression is one of its main adverse effects F"},{"label":"D","text":"It acts at opiod receptors T"},{"label":"E","text":"Is a metabolite of the antidepressant, trazodone T 42 Lidocaine:"},{"label":"A","text":"Is widely used for local anaesthesia T"},{"label":"B","text":"Has rapid action and medium duration of action T"},{"label":"C","text":"Has poor tissue penetration F(PROCAINE)"},{"label":"D","text":"It is also used intravenously for treating ventricular dysrhythmias T"},{"label":"E","text":"Has less tendency to cause CNS effects than Procaine T 43 Aminophylline:"},{"label":"A","text":"Is the main therapeutic drug of the Xanthine class of drugs T"},{"label":"B","text":"The relaxant effect on smooth muscle has been attributed to stimulation of the phosphodiesterase isoenzyme F"},{"label":"C","text":"Measurement of theophylline plasma concentration is useful for optmising aminophylline dosage T"},{"label":"D","text":"Its plasma concentration is decreased by erythromycin, ciprofloxacin or fluconazole F"},{"label":"E","text":"Is used as a second-line drug in addition to steroids in patients whose asthma does not rwspond to β2-adrenoceptor agonists F 44 Inhaled beclometasone:"},{"label":"A","text":"Its important action in asthma is to decrease the formation of Th1 cytokines T"},{"label":"B","text":"Is not commonly associated with serious unwanted effects T"},{"label":"C","text":"In some individuals, resistance may develop after prolonged use T"},{"label":"D","text":"May cause oropharyngeal candidiasis T"},{"label":"E","text":"Is indicated in asthma patients who require regular bronchodilators T 45 The following antifungal agents have been paired correctly with an appropriate indication:"},{"label":"A","text":"Nystatin – otal Candida infections T"},{"label":"B","text":"Griseofulvin – cryptoccal meningitis F(DERMATOPHYTE OF NAILS N SKIN)"},{"label":"C","text":"Flucytosine – tinea pedis F(CRYPT MENINGTS,SYS CANDID)"},{"label":"D","text":"Clotrimazole – intertrigo T(TOPICAL)"},{"label":"E","text":"Miconazole – cold sored F(INFEC OF GIT)"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","too_many_options_possible_merge","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Pharmacokinetics and Dynamics · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","long_option_manual_review","nonunique_option_labels","prior_structural_review_flag","too_many_options","too_many_options_possible_merge","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-98b38bab30e9","stem":"Zidovudine (AZT)","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Is not in any of the recommemnded first-line ARV regimens in infants and children in Tanzania"},{"label":"B","text":"Is administered by subcutaneous injection F"},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibits viral reverse transription T"},{"label":"D","text":"Should be stopped if cotrimoxazole is required T"},{"label":"E","text":"Is a non-nucloside reverse transcriptase inhibitor T"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"57f660cd89cb988d618f23967d7cb000f8a78b8a"},{"id":"q-ea8a117d3ff9","stem":"A new autonomic drug that does not enter the brain was given IV to healthy volunteers. The cardiovascular effects are tabulated below: The effects of the new drug are most similar to those of which of the following agents?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Dobutamine"},{"label":"B","text":"Epinephrine"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"D","text":"Norepinephrine"},{"label":"E","text":"Phenylephrine"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3c01013bba34","stem":"A 44-year-old man presents to his local doctor complaining of an impaired voluntary raising of the eyelid. Physical examination shows miosis, ptosis, flushed facial skin and loss of sweating over the left side of the body. The physician puts into the conjunctival sac of each eye one drop of cocaine, then (after washing) one drop of phenylephrine. The size of the pupils before and after the two treatments is shown in the table below. From the above data the physician concludes that there is a localized lesion in which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Postganglionic sympathetic nerves innervating the face"},{"label":"B","text":"Preganglionic sympathetic nerves innervating the face"},{"label":"C","text":"Postganglionic parasympathetic nerves innervating the face"},{"label":"D","text":"Preganglionic parasympathetic nerves innervating the face"},{"label":"E","text":"Medulla oblongata"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-444d3be53ba6","stem":"A new autonomic drug was tested on healthy volunteers. Subjects were treated with saline or with the drug and then the heart rate was measured under resting or exercise conditions. The results were the following: The tested drug most likely a drug from which of the following classes of autonomic drugs?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Beta-blocker"},{"label":"B","text":"Cholinergic agonist"},{"label":"C","text":"cholinergic antagonist"},{"label":"D","text":"Ganglionic blocker"},{"label":"E","text":"Alpha-1 blocker Parameter | Control | Peak drug effect Systolic BP (mm Hg) | 125 | 145 Diastolic BP (mm Hg) | 75 | 95 Heart rate (bpm) | 70 | 58 Stroke volume (mL) | 71.5 | 74.0 Ejection fraction | 60% | 60% | Pupil Size (mm) | Pupil Size (mm) Treatment | Right eye | Left eye Without treatment | 3 | 2.5 After cocaine | 3.5 | 2.5 After phenylephrine | 3.5 | 3.5"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-23032784b4c7","stem":"Both MAO and COMT typically act to metabolize circulating Epi and NE.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Adrenergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thus, the most abundant metabolite of Epi/NE found in the urine is:"},{"label":"B","text":"Other metablolites found in lesser amounts include:"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["mcq_missing_options","not_enough_options","prior_structural_review_flag"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-71a77eb66f25","stem":"Which of the following induces microsomal enzymes ...T............Phenobarbitone ...F............Ethanol ...F............Diazepam ...F............Ketaconazole ...T............Nevirapine SECTION III: Matching Match the members of group A to those of group B by choosing and writing (in the space provided). The letter of the most corresponding member of group B. Members of group B shall be used only once and some neither shall nor be used at all. Group A","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-453bd867cce5","stem":"1) Which of the following statements correctly matches the beta lactam antibiotic with the bacteria sensitive to it?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-894eb39501f1","stem":"A new synthetic drug is to be studied for its cardiovascular effects. The drug is given IV to four laboratory animals while the heart rate and blood pressure is recorded. The animals were pretreated as indicated below. The NET CHANGES (+ or -) induced by the new drug (not by the pretreatment) are shown in the following table. The drug under study is most similar to which of the following?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"Autonomic Pharmacology","subtopic":"Cholinergic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Norepinephrine"},{"label":"B","text":"Epinephrine"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoproterenol"},{"label":"D","text":"Phenylephrine"},{"label":"E","text":"Acetylcholine Drug | Pupillary diameter | Lens curvature | Intraocular Pressure A | + | 0 | - B | - | + | - C | 0 | 0 | - D | + | - | - E | + | - | + + = increased; - = decreased; 0 = negligible effect | + = increased; - = decreased; 0 = negligible effect | + = increased; - = decreased; 0 = negligible effect | + = increased; - = decreased; 0 = negligible effect | Pretreatment | Pretreatment | Pretreatment | Pretreatment | None | Prazosin | Propranolol | Atropine Change in heart rate (bpm) | -10 | ±2 | -10 | ±2 Change in diastolic blood pressure (mm Hg) | +20 | ±2 | +20 | +20"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Autonomic Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["long_option_manual_review"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4366011af984","stem":"Antimicrobial agent of choice for the outpatient treatment of infections due to animal bite wound be","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antimicrobial","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cefuroxime sodium"},{"label":"B","text":"Amoxicillin/clavulanate potassium"},{"label":"C","text":"Penicillin V"},{"label":"D","text":"Ampicillin sodium/Salbactam sodium"},{"label":"E","text":"Ticarcillin disodium/clavulanate potassium"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3861f22fd524","stem":"An antibiotic is distributed in total body water and has an elimination half life of 45 minutes. Which one of the following statements is consistant with that observation?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"The drug is stored in high concentration in fat"},{"label":"B","text":"The rapid rate of disappearance rules out metabolism of the drug by the liver"},{"label":"C","text":"The drug is actively transported into tubular urine"},{"label":"D","text":"The drug cannot be bound to plasma proteins"},{"label":"E","text":"The drug is eliminated only by glomerular filtration"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4f29b4e5fdb2","stem":"Which one of the following is indicated for treatment of chlamydial urethritis during pregnancy?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Amoxicillin (Amoxil)"},{"label":"B","text":"Penicillin V"},{"label":"C","text":"Erythromycin base"},{"label":"D","text":"Doxycycline (Monodox)"},{"label":"E","text":"Tetracycline"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-61aade7efcee","stem":"A 25-year-old male has a dental infection associated with facial swelling and lymphadenopathy. Which one of the following is the most appropriate antibiotic?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cephalexin"},{"label":"B","text":"Tetracycline"},{"label":"C","text":"Penicillin (note: watch an animation on penicillin mechanism of action)"},{"label":"D","text":"Erythromycin"},{"label":"E","text":"Gentamicin"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e96c9517f0da","stem":"A 16-year-old sexually active nulliparous white female complains of pelvic pain and vaginal discharge. On examination she is found to have a temperature of 39.8° C (102.0° F), pain with movement of the cervix, and tenderness and a mass in the right adnexa. Which one of the following treatment would be appropriate?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Outpatient treatment with penicillin G procaine ,intramuscularly; probenecid orally; plus doxycycline (Vibramycin) orally for 14 days and reexamination in 3 days"},{"label":"B","text":"Outpatient treatment with ceftriaxone intramuscularly; probenecid orally; plus doxycycline twice a day for 14 days and reexamination in 1 week"},{"label":"C","text":"Outpatient treatment with cefoxitin intramuscularly; plus doxycycline twice a day for 14 days and reexamination in 10 days"},{"label":"D","text":"Hospitalization for treatment with cefoxitin intravenously and doxycycline orally or intravenously, then doxycycline orally twice a day to complete 14 days of treatment"},{"label":"E","text":"Erythromycin 2 grams orally as a single dose"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-250d49c5dbdd","stem":"A drug that may cause nephrotoxicity, is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penicillin G"},{"label":"B","text":"Erythrocin"},{"label":"C","text":"Gentamycin"},{"label":"D","text":"Cefuroxime"},{"label":"E","text":"Penicillin V"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4bfa27604231","stem":"Which one of the following is a common early side effect of Penicillin?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antimicrobials","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Constipation"},{"label":"B","text":"Loss of appetite"},{"label":"C","text":"Orthostatic hypotension"},{"label":"D","text":"Atrioventricular block"},{"label":"E","text":"Skin rash"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"provisional_independently_reasoned","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"provisional_answer","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-82f50c2615e5","stem":"Which of the following might be seen in patient of TB, who has regularly been injecting intramuscular injection of streptomycin","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Depression"},{"label":"B","text":"Sialorrhea"},{"label":"C","text":"Increased serum alanine aminotransferase"},{"label":"D","text":"Priapism"},{"label":"E","text":"Deafness"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-5a36163afbdc","stem":"Significant negative interaction has been known to occur between the following drugs if given concurrently","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Penicillin G & Penicillin V"},{"label":"B","text":"Penicillin & tetracycline,"},{"label":"C","text":"Penicillin & amp; Gentamycin"},{"label":"D","text":"Penicillin & Clavulanic acid"},{"label":"E","text":"Ticarcillin & Clavulanic acid"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bec017baaec4","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a recognized complication of ampicillin toxicity","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Hemolytic anemia"},{"label":"B","text":"Diarrhea"},{"label":"C","text":"Nausea"},{"label":"D","text":"Overgrowth of gram-positive organisms"},{"label":"E","text":"Ventricular fibrillation"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e5ef468d738a","stem":"Doctor is wrong when he suggests his patient to get drug","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptomycin by IV route"},{"label":"B","text":"Gentamycin by IV route"},{"label":"C","text":"Neomycin by oral route"},{"label":"D","text":"Penicillin V by oral route"},{"label":"E","text":"Cefepime by IV route"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0815421bd1b1","stem":"Doxycycline is used","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"for Pneumonia as drug of 1st choice"},{"label":"B","text":"for the prevention of TB"},{"label":"C","text":"for the prevention of malaria"},{"label":"D","text":"for the prevention of leprosy"},{"label":"E","text":"for the treatment of sexually transmitted diseases"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-89213dbfb53e","stem":"Which of the following statement is true","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"nafcillin is beta-lactamase resistant antibiotic"},{"label":"B","text":"cefazolin is second generation cephalosporin"},{"label":"C","text":"tazobactam is used in gonorrhea mainly"},{"label":"D","text":"penicillin G is contraindicated in infections caused by spirochetes"},{"label":"E","text":"thrombocytosis is main side effect of Linezolid"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-69f012474e1f","stem":"All of the following statement is true, except","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"chloramphenicol is broad spectrum protein synthesis inhibitor"},{"label":"B","text":"linezolid and streptogramins are narrow spectrum protein synthesis inhibitors"},{"label":"C","text":"mechanism of resistance to chloramphenicol is plasmid mediated and occurs through the formation of acetyltransferases that inactivate the drug"},{"label":"D","text":"tetracyclines are narrow spectrum protein synthesis inhibitors"},{"label":"E","text":"susceptible organisms accumulate tetracyclines intracellularly via energy dependant transport systems in their cell membranes"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6494b6377add","stem":"in a patient with culture-positive enterococcal endocarditis who has failed to respond to vancomycin because of resistance, the treatment most likely to be effective is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"clarithromicin"},{"label":"B","text":"erythromycin"},{"label":"C","text":"linezolid"},{"label":"D","text":"minocycline"},{"label":"E","text":"Ticarcillin"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c78071666960","stem":"A 24 year old male patient is suffering from peptic ulcer. He was advised anti-peptic ulcer drugs including doxycycline. Which one of the following statements about doxycycline is false?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"it is bacteriostatic"},{"label":"B","text":"it is excreted mainly in the feces"},{"label":"C","text":"it is used in Lyme disease"},{"label":"D","text":"it has a long elimination half-life"},{"label":"E","text":"it is more active than tetracycline against H. Pylori"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1e2fa14e86f9","stem":"A 12 years old female patient came to pediatrician suffering from headache, high grade fever, moderate chest pain, joint pain and drowsy. When doctor got history, he was told to take diazepam 2.5 mg by patient 15 hours ago. Actually it was already a diagnosed case of pneumonia and she was on drug treatment including streptogramin. Concerning streptogramins, which one of the following statements is false","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"they are active against methicillin-resistant staphylococci"},{"label":"B","text":"they may cause a syndrome of arthralgia and myalgia"},{"label":"C","text":"they are used in the management of infections caused by vancomycin-resistant enterococci"},{"label":"D","text":"they are associated with post antibiotic effect"},{"label":"E","text":"they induce formation of hepatic drug-metabolizing enzymes"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-be70e1e23057","stem":"Which statement is true about tetracyclines?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"resistance mechanisms include decreased activity of the uptake systems and, most importantly, the development of mechanisms like efflux pumps for active extrusion of tetracyclines"},{"label":"B","text":"is contraindicated in gram-positive bacterial infections"},{"label":"C","text":"is absolutely contraindicated in gram-negative bacterial infections"},{"label":"D","text":"mechanism of action of tetracycline is same as Clindamycin"},{"label":"E","text":"resistant organisms are killed when tetracycline is used in large doses"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-42a77d187898","stem":"A 33 years old male patient has been diagnosed mycoplsma pneumoniae infection. He was treated by one of the broad spectrum antibacterial drug group tetracycline 2 gram TID for 21 days. What may be most appropriate toxicity?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antimicrobials","options":[{"label":"A","text":"anemia"},{"label":"B","text":"hepatic necrosis"},{"label":"C","text":"thrombocytosis"},{"label":"D","text":"alopecia"},{"label":"E","text":"sleeplessness"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d054b6c44d7a","stem":"an elderly debilitated patient has a fever believed to be due to an infection. He has extensive skin lesions, scrapings of which reveal the presence of large numbers of gram-positive cocci. The most appropriate drug to use for treatment of this patient is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"amoxicillin"},{"label":"B","text":"salbactam"},{"label":"C","text":"cefoxitin"},{"label":"D","text":"nafcillin"},{"label":"E","text":"penicillin G"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e0c3987bfe5d","stem":"A 21 year old man was seen in a clinic with a complaint of dysuria and urethral discharge of yellow pus. He has a painless clean-base ulcer on the penis and nontender enlargement of the regional lymph nodes. Gram stain of the urethral exudates showed gram negative diplococci within polymorphonucleocytes. The patient informed the clinic staff that he was unemployed and had not eaten a meal for two days. The most appropriate treatment of gonorrhea in this patient is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"amoxicillin orally for 7 days"},{"label":"B","text":"vancomycin intramuscularly as a single dose"},{"label":"C","text":"ceftriaxone intramuscularly as a single dose"},{"label":"D","text":"tetracycline orally for 7 days"},{"label":"E","text":"procaine penicillin G intramuscularly as a single dose plus one gram of probenecid"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1e177ae3b767","stem":"Eighty years old male patient having 73 Kg body weight was suffering from fever, headache and lumbar pain since last 7 days. He was examined in clinic. Gram stain of the smear of CSF revealed gram positive rods resembling diphtheroids. The antibiotic regimen for empiric treatment would include","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Erythromycin"},{"label":"B","text":"Ticarcillin"},{"label":"C","text":"Cefotetan"},{"label":"D","text":"Cefazolin"},{"label":"E","text":"Ampicillin"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-40c95ca36db0","stem":"A 25 year old male presents to the medical OPD with complaint of cough and low grade fever since the last 3 months. Laboratory tests show sputum positive for acid fast bacteria. Which of the following combination of drugs is most likely to be administered at the start to this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptomycin, Isoniazid, Rifampicin and Pyrazinamide"},{"label":"B","text":"PAS, Pyrazinamide and Rifampicin"},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol and Isoniazid"},{"label":"D","text":"Streptomycin and Rifampicin"},{"label":"E","text":"Streptomycin alone"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-6b81f1ecc442","stem":"A 25 year old male has been given treatment for pulmonary tuberculosis for 2 months as initial phase. Now he comes to the OPD for medication for the continuation phase. Which of the following drugs will he most likely be given for the continuation phase?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Pyrazinamide and Isoniazid"},{"label":"B","text":"Ethambutol and Streptomycin"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoniazid and Rifampicin"},{"label":"D","text":"Rifampicin and Streptomycin"},{"label":"E","text":"Ethambutol and Pyrazinamide"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b3c6965f2372","stem":"A young man 26 years old is started treated for pulmonary tuberculosis. He comes to the medical OPD after 1 week with complaint of orange red colored urine and sweat and tears. Which of the following drugs is most likely causing these symptoms?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptomycin"},{"label":"B","text":"Rifampicin"},{"label":"C","text":"Ethambutol"},{"label":"D","text":"Pyrazinamide"},{"label":"E","text":"Thiacetazone"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d3b43135dc41","stem":"A 30 year old male with known TB infection and taking medication since 1 month comes to the Eye OPD with complaint of visual disturbances including decreased visibility and slight color blindness. If these symptoms are due to his drug treatment, which one of the following drugs is most likely the cause of his symptoms?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptomycin"},{"label":"B","text":"Ciprofloxacin"},{"label":"C","text":"Ethambutol"},{"label":"D","text":"Rifampicin"},{"label":"E","text":"Isoniazid"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9066df0cb930","stem":"A 35 year old male recently diagnosed with tuberculosis and is started on medication. After a few days he comes to the emergency department a few hours after taking a meal at McDonald’s with severe pain in the big toe. Blood tests show a very high uric acid level. Which of the following drugs most likely caused these symptoms?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Streptomycin"},{"label":"B","text":"Rifampicin"},{"label":"C","text":"Isoniazid"},{"label":"D","text":"Amikacin"},{"label":"E","text":"Pyrazinamide"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8bd745dc7d11","stem":"A 40 year old male taking medication for tuberculosis since the last 2 months comes to the ENT OPD with complaint of hearing problems and ringing in the years. The drug with which of the following mechanisms of action is most likely causing these symptoms?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Binds to 30 S Ribosome subunit & inhibits initiation complex"},{"label":"B","text":"Inhibits DNA Dependent RNA Polymerase"},{"label":"C","text":"Inhibits synthesis of mycolic acid"},{"label":"D","text":"Inhibits synthesis of arabinoglycan subunits"},{"label":"E","text":"Inhibits DNA Gyrase"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fd84001d989e","stem":"A 35 year old female taking medication for tuberculosis and oral contraceptives comes to the Gynaecology OPD with complaint of amenorrhea. Laboratory test shows a positive pregnancy test. Which one of the following drugs most likely caused her to become pregnant?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Endocrine Pharmacology · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Endocrine Pharmacology","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Rifampicin"},{"label":"B","text":"Pyrazinamide"},{"label":"C","text":"Streptomycin"},{"label":"D","text":"Ethambutol"},{"label":"E","text":"Isoniazid"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Endocrine Pharmacology · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7c68f1eaa11e","stem":"A 45 year old male with diagnosed chronic liver disease gets infected with the tuberculosis. Which one of the following drugs would be most safe for this patient for the treatment of tuberculosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Isoniazid"},{"label":"B","text":"Rifampin"},{"label":"C","text":"Pyrazinamide"},{"label":"D","text":"Ethambutol"},{"label":"E","text":"Ethionamide"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f362e867c0ff","stem":"A 48 year old male is diagnosed with tuberculosis and has been treated for 2 months with isoniazid and rifampicin with good response. But the patient develops numbness and paresthesias in the extremities. What will you most likely do next?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antimicrobials","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Add pyridoxine"},{"label":"B","text":"Stop isoniazid"},{"label":"C","text":"Add vitamin E"},{"label":"D","text":"Replace rifampin with ethambutol"},{"label":"E","text":"Replace isoniazid with streptomycin"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-d074175c05d6","stem":"A 30 year old male presents with history of fever occurring in a step-ladder fashion for the last 10 days. He also feels abdominal pain after taking meals. Lab tests show decreased TLC count and a positive Widal test. The drug which is most likely to be given acts by which one of the following mechanisms?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ciprofloxacin"},{"label":"B","text":"Ampicillin"},{"label":"C","text":"Cotrimoxazole"},{"label":"D","text":"Cefixime"},{"label":"E","text":"Azithromycin"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-110be54de0fb","stem":"A 35 year old pregnant woman presents to the gynecology department with complaint of pain with rising fever since the last 5 days. Lab tests show gram negative bacilli and widal test comes out positive. Which one of the following drugs will most likely be administered?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ciprofloxacin"},{"label":"B","text":"Levofloxacin"},{"label":"C","text":"Ofloxacin"},{"label":"D","text":"Norfloxacin"},{"label":"E","text":"Ampicillin"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9ac1c33ffaf6","stem":"A 27 year old male football player gets injured in a match. He comes to the emergency department with pain and swelling near the ankle. On examination, there is small wound with a tender swelling near the ankle joint. Further tests reveal tendon rupture with tendonitis setting in. Which one of the following antibiotics, if required, will most probably not be given to this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ceftriaxone"},{"label":"B","text":"Clarithromycin"},{"label":"C","text":"Aminoglycoside"},{"label":"D","text":"Ciprofloxacin"},{"label":"E","text":"Amoxicillin"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-4dd88a1e278e","stem":"A 40 year old male comes to the medical OPD with symptoms of cough & fever for the last few days. Sputum culture shows presence of gram positive cocci. Which one of the following Fluoroquinolones is most likely to be administered to this patient?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Antimicrobials","options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ciprofloxacin"},{"label":"B","text":"Norfloxacin"},{"label":"C","text":"Ofloxacin"},{"label":"D","text":"Gatifloxacin"},{"label":"E","text":"Levofloxacin"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-883f6752f4cb","stem":"A 50 year old patient comes to the emergency department, a day after he has been administered an antibiotic for an on-going infection, with complaint of palpitations. ECG reveals prolonged QT interval. Which one of the following Fluoroquinolones most likely caused this adverse effect?","type":"ECG Interpretation","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Antimicrobials","topicOriginal":"Antimicrobials","subtopic":"Antibiotic","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Ciprofloxacin"},{"label":"B","text":"Norfloxacin"},{"label":"C","text":"Levofloxacin"},{"label":"D","text":"Ofloxacin"},{"label":"E","text":"Gatifloxacin"}],"priority":0,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Antimicrobials","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-b315099b37f9","stem":"Disinfectants are","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Strong chemical agents that inhibit or kill micro-organisms"},{"label":"B","text":"Agents with sufficient toxicity for host cells"},{"label":"C","text":"They kill both vegetative cells and spores"},{"label":"D","text":"A process intended to kill or remove all types of micro-organisms including spores and include viruses"},{"label":"E","text":"A process that kills non-sporulating microorganisms by hot water or steam at 65-100 C"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-dfa870fd12da","stem":"A patient with watery stools is diagnosed as suffering from amebic dysentery. He is given a drug that causes a metalic taste in the mouth which drug may be given.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Iodoquinol"},{"label":"B","text":"Diloxanide furate"},{"label":"C","text":"Metronidazole"},{"label":"D","text":"Pentamiline"},{"label":"E","text":"Emetine."}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-0534b1052667","stem":"The reason for giving Metronidazole for oropharyngeal infection is due to its good activity against","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Gram positive cocci"},{"label":"B","text":"Gram Negative cocci"},{"label":"C","text":"Gram positive bacilli"},{"label":"D","text":"Gram negative bacilli"},{"label":"E","text":"Anaerobes like B. fragilis"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["scientific_ambiguity_multiple_plausible_options","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-fe27172d70f8","stem":"Patient comes to emergency with the complaints of marked visual and auditory abnormities, vomiting diarrhea abdominal pain & skin rashes. From the history of the patient it was revealed that he took some drug for the treatment of fever. What could be probable diagnosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Cinchonism"},{"label":"B","text":"Acute gastro enteritis"},{"label":"C","text":"Cholera"},{"label":"D","text":"Typhoid fever"},{"label":"E","text":"Acid peptic disease"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-115a98a33857","stem":"A 37 year old male is having repeated episodes of fever with chills with blood smear positive for malaria since last one year. He was given chloroquine each time & the fever subsided. Which of the following drugs would you add with chloroquine this time?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Artemether"},{"label":"B","text":"Mefloquine"},{"label":"C","text":"Malarone"},{"label":"D","text":"Primaquine"},{"label":"E","text":"Halofantrine"}],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1377349c54a4","stem":"2005 Drug Receptors and Pharmacodynamics (how drugs work on the body) The action of a drug on the body, including receptor interactions, dose-response phenomena, and mechanisms of therapeutic and toxic action. [Slide 4] Drug action The main ways by which drugs act are via interaction with cell proteins, namely receptors, ion channels, enzymes and transport/carrier proteins. In addition, drugs can work by themselves mechanically or chemically. Its useful to know what are the basic principles of drug action. [Slide 5] Principles of Drug action Stimulation: Enhancement of the level of a specific biological activity (usually already ongoing physiological process). E.g. adrenaline stimulates heart rate. Depression: Diminution of the level of a specific biological activity (usually already ongoing physiological process). E.g. barbiturate depress the CNS. Replacement: Replacement of the natural hormones or enzymes (any substance) which are deficient in our body. E.g. insulin for treating diabetes. Cytotoxic action: Toxic effects on invading micro organisms or cancer cells. [Slide 6] How does all this happen?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-bef950b18eb5","stem":"2005 DR Complex Effect [Slide 34] Drug (D) - Receptor (R) Interaction Kd = ([D] * [R]) / [DR] = k-1/k1 k1 k-1 D + R DR Kd = dissociation constant k1 = association rate constant K-1= dissociation rate constant [Slide 35] Assessment of Receptor Occupation Measure of Affinity kd is a relative measure of affinity of a drug for its receptor. It varies inversely with the affinity of the drug for its receptor High-affinity drugs have lower kd values and occupy a greater number of drug receptors than drugs with lower affinities. [Slide 36] What can we learn?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"General Pharmacology","topicOriginal":"General Pharmacology","subtopic":"Receptor","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"General Pharmacology","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem"],"cleaningOperations":[],"duplicateStemGroup":"7a04f6cf95e3ca530ca3cc4522849652117354f6"},{"id":"q-49284be2c8f1","stem":"Which of the following drugs is(are) known to induce hyperuricemia and occasionally to bring about an acute gouty attack?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmacology","topic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","topicOriginal":"Cardiovascular Drugs","subtopic":"Antihypertensive","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":0,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pharmacology","displayTopic":"Cardiovascular Drugs","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a235367f5e85","stem":"Which of the following diuretics has a greater antihypertensive activity?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Pharmaco